window.__GNAMMA_SANANDAJI84.push(...[{"id":"I256.lower.03","volume":1,"page":256,"physical_pdf_page":269,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله لتبدل السطوح المحيطة الخ) أقول إن اكتفوا في الحركة بتبدل بعض السطوح يلزمهم القول بحركة المسافة التي يتحرك المتحرك فيها ولا قائل به وإن اشترطوا فيها تبدل جميع السطوح المحيطة فلا نسلم تحققه نحو الحجر المستقر على الأرض في الماء الجاري فافهم","english":"On “Because the enclosing surfaces change, etc.” I say: if a change in some of the surfaces suffices for motion, they must affirm motion of the distance through which a moving thing moves, which no one maintains. If they require all the enclosing surfaces to change, we do not concede that this occurs in a stone resting on the ground in flowing water, for example. Understand.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I256.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I256.lower.04","volume":1,"page":256,"physical_pdf_page":269,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله بخلافه على الأول الخ) فإن باطن المتحرك متحرك والواقف ساكن على الأول وقد يستدل على ذلك بأن الباطن لو كان ساكنا مع حركة باقي الأجزاء للزم الانفكاك وكذا أن الواقف لو كان متحركا بتبدل السطوح لزم تحركه في حالة واحدة إلى جهتين مختلفتين عند اختلاف السطوح المحيطة لكن الكل ضعيف","english":"On “Otherwise on the first account, etc.” On the first account, the interior of the moving body moves and the stationary body rests. It may be argued that, if the interior remained at rest while the other parts moved, the body’s parts would separate. Likewise, if the stationary body moved because its enclosing surfaces changed, it would move in two different directions simultaneously when those surfaces changed differently. All these arguments, however, are weak.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I256.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I256.upper.01","volume":1,"page":256,"physical_pdf_page":269,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"كالحجر المستقر على الأرض في الماء الجاري (ساكن) بشهادة العرف (ومبنى التردد) في ذلك (على التردد في حقيقة الحيز) أهو البعد الذي ينفذ فيه بعد الجسم أو السطح الباطن وعلى الثاني باطن المتحرك ساكن لعدم مفارقته عن السطح الباطن والواقف متحرك لتبدل السطوح المحيطة به بخلافه على الأول كما لا يخفى","english":"—as with a stone resting on the ground in flowing water—“is at rest,” as ordinary usage attests. “The basis of the uncertainty” here “is uncertainty about the reality of spatial region”: is it the extension into which the body’s extension penetrates, or the inner surface [of the containing body]? On the second account, the interior of a moving body is at rest because it does not leave the inner surface, while a stationary body moves because its enclosing surfaces change. On the first account the position is otherwise, as is evident.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I256.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I255.upper.02","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I256.upper.02","volume":1,"page":256,"physical_pdf_page":269,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(وقول الفلاسفة الحركة خروج من القوة إلى الفعل تدريجا وليس) خروجا (يسيرا) بل تدريجا (أولا دفعة) بل دفعات والخروج دفعة لا يسمى حركة بل كونا وفسادا (مبني) هذا القول منهم (على بداهة تصور هذه المعاني) المتناوبة في الذكر في تفسير الحركة وأنها واضحة عند العقل من غير احتياج إلى تصور الزمان المفتقر إلى تصور الحركة فلا يرد أن معنى التدريج أن لا يكون دفعة","english":"“The philosophers’ statement that motion is emergence from potentiality into actuality gradually”—not merely a “slight” emergence, but a gradual one—“or not all at once,” rather in successive occurrences, rests “upon the self-evidence of conceiving these meanings” that are used interchangeably to explain motion. An emergence all at once is not called motion, but generation and corruption. These meanings are clear to the intellect without requiring the concept of time, which itself depends upon the concept of motion. The objection therefore does not arise that “gradually” means “not all at once”—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I256.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I257.upper.01","readings":[{"locus":"وليس يسيرا / بل دفعات","status":"The inserted clarification concerning slight emergence and successive occurrences is cramped in the scan; retain provisionally pending second proof."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I257.lower.01","volume":1,"page":257,"physical_pdf_page":270,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله هو طرف الزمان الخ) فيكون التعريف دوريا (قوله معان لهذه الخ) أي لا يتوقف تصور تلك المعاني على تصور الزمان المتوقف على تصور الحركة حتى يدور بل إنما يتوقف تحققها على تحققها فيمكن معرفتها لوضوحها من غير افتقار إلى تصور الزمان والحركة وهذا في التدريج واليسير واضح وأما في اللادفعة فلأن تصور اللادفعة وإن كان متوقفا على تصور الدفعة لكن يمكن تصورها من غير تصورهما أيضا","english":"On “Which is a boundary of time, etc.”: the definition would then be circular. On the statement about these meanings: conceiving them does not depend upon conceiving time, which depends upon conceiving motion, so no circle results. It is only their realisation that depends upon the realisation of those [temporal conditions]. Their clarity makes it possible to know them without first conceiving time and motion. This is evident in gradualness and slightness. As for “not all at once,” although conceiving it depends upon conceiving “all at once,” the latter too can be conceived without conceiving time and motion.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I257.jpg","stage":69,"readings":[{"locus":"قوله معان لهذه / تحققها على تحققها","status":"The quoted lemma and repeated pronouns are indistinct. The printed correction below itself marks this passage as problematic."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I257.lower.02","volume":1,"page":257,"physical_pdf_page":270,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"ولتوهم الدور من هذا التعريف فسرها بعضهم بأنها كمال أول لجسم بالنظر لما هو بالقوة من حيث هو بالقوة والمراد حصول ما لم يكن حاصلا ولا خفاء في أن الحركة أمر ممكن الحصول للجسم فيكون حصولها كمالا واحترز بقيد الأولية من الوصول فإن الجسم إذا كان في مكان وأمكن حصوله في مكان آخر كان له إمكانان أحدهما إمكان الوصول أي حصوله في المكان الآخر والآخر إمكان التوجه إليه والتوجه مقدم على الوصول فهي كمال أول والوصول كمال ثان فلا بد في الحركة من مطلوب يمكن الوصول إليه لكون توجه المتحرك نحوه ومن أن يبقى من ذلك التوجه شيء بالقوة إذ لا توجه بعد الوصول فتكون الحركة كمالا للمتحرك من جهة المبدأ لا الحصول فذلك المطلوب فهي كمال أول من جهة ماله بالقوة من حيث إنه بالقوة لا بالفعل","english":"Because this definition appears circular, some explain motion as a first actuality of a body with respect to what it possesses potentially, insofar as it is potential. “Actuality” here means the acquisition of what was not previously acquired. Motion is plainly something a body can acquire, so acquiring it is an actuality. “First” excludes arrival. A body situated in one place and capable of occupying another has two possibilities: arriving, that is, being present in the other place; and proceeding toward it. Proceeding precedes arrival, so it is a first actuality and arrival a second. Motion therefore requires an attainable goal toward which the moving thing proceeds, and requires something of that proceeding to remain potential, since there is no further proceeding after arrival. Motion is thus an actuality of the moving thing from the aspect of commencing [toward the goal], not of acquiring that goal. It is a first actuality with respect to what it possesses potentially, insofar as it is potential and not actual.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I257.jpg","stage":69,"readings":[{"locus":"من جهة المبدأ لا الحصول فذلك المطلوب","status":"The connection before ذلك المطلوب is insecure; the distinction between proceeding and completed attainment is clear."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I257.lower.03","volume":1,"page":257,"physical_pdf_page":270,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"إن قيل لا يصدق هذا التفسير على الحركة المستديرة إذ لا منتهى لها بالفعل فلا يتحقق هناك أولية وثانوية أجيب بأن كل نقطة تفرض قبال المتحرك على الاستدارة فالنسبة إليها من حيث طلبها توجه فيكون كمالا أولا ومن حيث الحصول عندها وصول فيكون كمالا ثانيا","english":"If it is objected that this explanation does not apply to circular motion, since circular motion has no actual endpoint and hence no firstness and secondness, the reply is: take any point ahead of the circularly moving thing. Relative to seeking that point there is proceeding, a first actuality; relative to being at it there is arrival, a second actuality.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I257.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I257.paratext.01","volume":1,"page":257,"physical_pdf_page":270,"layer":"paratext","attribution":"printer","arabic":"* ١٧ - تقريب أول *","english":"Printer’s gathering marker: “17 — Taqrīb, first [volume].”","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I257.jpg","stage":69,"editorial":"Non-argumentative print furniture retained separately; not counted as a translated commentary unit.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I257.ruled.01","volume":1,"page":257,"physical_pdf_page":270,"layer":"ruled","attribution":"corrector identified by function only","arabic":"(١) قوله وأما في اللادفعة إلخ في العبارة شيء تأمل كتبه مصححه","english":"(1) On “As for ‘not all at once,’ etc.” There is something problematic in the expression. Reflect. Written by its corrector.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I257.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I257.upper.01","volume":1,"page":257,"physical_pdf_page":270,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"ومعنى الحصول دفعة أن يكون في آن هو طرف الزمان المقدر بالحركة وذلك لأن الآن والزمان يسبقان لهذه المعاني في الوجود لا في التصور (والموجود منها) أي من الحركة عندهم (كون الجسم متوسطا بين المبدأ والمنتهى) اللذين للمسافة لا للحركة","english":"—and “occurring all at once” means occurring at an instant that is a boundary of time measured by motion. For instant and time precede these meanings in existence, not in conception. “What exists of motion,” according to them, “is the body’s being intermediate between beginning and end”—the beginning and end of the distance, not of the motion.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I257.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I256.upper.02","join_out":"I258.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I258.lower.01","volume":1,"page":258,"physical_pdf_page":271,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله حتى يلزم الدور الخ) بل لا معنى لكون الجسم متوسطا بين مبدأ الحركة ومنتهاها إنما المتوسط بينهما حركة الجسم لا الجسم فإن الجسم إنما هو متوسط بين مبدأ المسافة ومنتهاها لكن لا يخفى أن المراد من مبدأ الحركة كما يأتي جزء المسافة الذي منه ابتداء الحركة وكذا المراد من منتهاها جزء المسافة الذي إليه تنتهي الحركة وليس المراد منهما ابتداء الحركة وانتهاءها فحينئذ يصح القول بتوسط الجسم وكذا حركته بينهما فيكون المراد من قوله لا للحركة الخ نفي اعتبار إضافتها إليها فتدبر جدا","english":"On “So that no circle results, etc.” Indeed, it makes no sense to say that the body is intermediate between the beginning and end of motion: what is intermediate between those is the body’s motion, not the body. The body is intermediate between the beginning and end of the distance. Nevertheless, “the beginning of motion,” as will follow, means the part of the distance from which motion begins, and its “end” means the part of the distance at which it terminates—not the motion’s beginning and ending themselves. It is then correct to say that both the body and its motion are intermediate between them. “Not of the motion, etc.” must therefore mean that their relation to motion is not included in the definition. Reflect very carefully.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I258.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I258.lower.02","volume":1,"page":258,"physical_pdf_page":271,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"ثم قال في شرح المقاصد في تفسير الحركة بهذا المعنى إنها كيفية بها يكون الجسم متوسطا بين المبدأ والمنتهى أقول هذا خلاف ما اشتهر من أنها نفس الكون في الوسط لكنه أنسب بما عليه أكثر الفلاسفة من عدم أنها من مقولة الكيف بخلاف ما اشتهر فإنه إنما يلائم مذهب القائلين بأنها من مقولة ما وقعت","english":"In Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid he then explains motion in this sense as a quality through which the body is intermediate between beginning and end. I say: this differs from the familiar account that it is the state of intermediacy itself. It is, however, more appropriate to the view of most philosophers that it belongs to the category of quality, whereas the familiar account accords only with the view that motion belongs to the category in which it occurs—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I258.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I259.lower.01","readings":[{"locus":"من عدم أنها من مقولة الكيف","status":"Read provisionally as من عدهم أنها من مقولة الكيف (“their counting it in quality”), not a denial. The image wording must be rechecked; English follows the explicit contrast."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I258.ruled.01","volume":1,"page":258,"physical_pdf_page":271,"layer":"ruled","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله بالنسبة إلى حدود المسافة) فإنه يتوهم من ذلك أنه أمر قابل للقسمة ذو أجزاء متصلة اه منه مد ظله (قوله بمعنى القطع) لقطع المسافة بها اه منه","english":"On “Relative to the boundaries of the distance”: this suggests that it is divisible, with connected parts. End. [Signed:] “From him, may his protection endure.” On “In the sense of traversal”: because the distance is traversed by it. End. [Signed:] “From him.”","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I258.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I258.upper.01","volume":1,"page":258,"physical_pdf_page":271,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"حتى يلزم الدور (على الاستمرار) دون الاستقرار في حيز واحد سواء كان منتقلا عنه أو إليه فإنا قيدنا الحركة بذلك أما مع الأول فظاهر وأما مع الثاني فلأنه لو استقر المتحرك بعد المبدأ في حيز لكان حاصلا في المنتهى لا متوسطا بينه وبين المبدأ (وأما كيفيته) أي الكون المذكور (المعقولة) تلك الكلية بواسطة استمراره وسيلانه بالنسبة إلى حدود المسافة (المتصلة الممتدة) من أول المسافة إلى منتهاها (فموهومة) لا وجود لها في الأعيان لا قبل وصول المتحرك إلى المنتهى لعدم تمامها وإذا انتهى فقد انقطعت وتسمى حركة بمعنى القطع والكون المذكور حركة بمعنى التوسط","english":"—so that no circle results. This intermediacy is “continuous,” without settling in a single spatial region, whether the motion is described as away from or toward that region. We have qualified motion thus. In the former case the reason is evident. In the latter, if the moving body settled in a region after the beginning, it would be at the endpoint, not intermediate between it and the beginning. “As for its conceived configuration”—the total configuration of the aforesaid state, conceived through its continuity and flow relative to the boundaries of the distance—“connected and extended” from the distance’s beginning to its end, “it is imagined.” It has no extramental existence: not before arrival, since it is not yet complete; and once arrival occurs, it has ceased. This is called motion in the sense of traversal. The state previously mentioned is motion in the sense of intermediacy.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I258.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I257.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I258.upper.02","volume":1,"page":258,"physical_pdf_page":271,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"ثم اعلم أن معنى الحركة بمعنى التوسط ووجودها في الخارج على اتصال الأحياز وعدم تفاصلها بناء على نفي الجزء وعدم تركيب الجسم والمسافة من الأجزاء أن على تقدير التركيب لو انتقل جوهر من جوهر","english":"Know, moreover, that the meaning of motion as intermediacy and its external existence [depend] upon the continuity of spatial regions and the absence of discrete separation between them, on the basis of denying indivisible parts and denying the composition of body and distance from such parts. For, on the supposition of composition, if an indivisible substance passes from one [adjacent] substance—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I258.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I259.upper.01","readings":[{"locus":"أن معنى الحركة بمعنى التوسط ووجودها في الخارج على اتصال","status":"The linking expression after اعلم is awkward in the print; “depend” is an explanatory supply, not an asserted source word."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I259.lower.01","volume":1,"page":259,"physical_pdf_page":272,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"فيه الحركة كالمتكلمين الذاهبين إلى أنها من مقولة الأين فافهم ثم قالوا إن وجودها بهذا المعنى يشهد به الحس لكن أورد عليه أن الحكم بالوجود في الخارج إما أن يكون على الماضي من الحركة أو الآتي أو الحاضر والكل باطل أما الماضي والآتي فظاهر وأما الحاضر فلأنه إن لم يكن منقسما لزم الجزء وإن كان منقسما عاد الكلام وأجيب بما مر في الزمان من أنا لا نسلم أنه لا وجود للماضي والآتي منها غاية الأمر أنه لا وجود لهما في الحال وهو لا يستلزم العدم مطلقا وأنت قد سمعت منا هناك ما يخدشه بل نقول خدشته ههنا أظهر مما هناك إذ لم يمكن أن يسلم وجود الزمان لا في الزمان لكن لا يمكن تسليم وجود الحركة لا فيه كما هو واضح","english":"—as with the theologians who hold motion to belong to “where.” Understand. They then say that the existence of motion in this sense is attested by sense. An objection is raised: the assertion of external existence concerns either past, future or present motion. All are untenable. The first two are plainly so. The present is either indivisible, entailing an indivisible part, or divisible, in which case the question recurs. The answer is the one already given concerning time: we do not concede that past and future motion do not exist. At most they do not exist now, which does not entail absolute nonexistence. You have already heard our criticism of that answer. Indeed, its weakness is more evident here: although one may concede that time exists without being in time, motion’s existence outside time cannot be conceded, as is clear.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I259.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I258.lower.02","readings":[{"locus":"إذ لم يمكن أن يسلم","status":"The connective appears to require “although it is possible to concede”; the source glyphs should be checked. English distinguishes the evident time/motion contrast without silently asserting a secure amended Arabic."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I259.lower.02","volume":1,"page":259,"physical_pdf_page":272,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله كما حققه بعض المحققين الخ) أقول لا فرق في هذا بين كون المسافة مركبة من أجزاء لا تتجزأ أو متصلا واحدا وذلك لأنها لا تكون موجودة مع الانقسام بالفعل لأن وجودها معه يوجب اجتماع أجزائها في الوجود وهو خلاف مقتضاها الذي هو عدم الاستقرار فحينئذ المتحرك بهذه الحركة إذا انتقل من جزء مفروض من المسافة إلى جزء آخر منها متصل بالجزء الأول بحيث لا يكون بينهما جزء بالفعل فقد حصلت الحركة من غير أن يكون للمتحرك توسط بين المبدأ والمنتهى إذ لا جزء بالفعل بينهما حتى يكون المتحرك عنده واقفا في الوسط سواء اعتبر بينهما حد مشترك كما هو رأي الفلاسفة أولا كما هو رأي المتكلمين والتمسك في ذلك بقبول كل قسمة وهمية كما يأتي في","english":"On “As certain investigators have established, etc.” I say: in this respect it makes no difference whether the distance is composed of indivisible parts or is one continuum. Motion cannot exist together with an actual division [into simultaneously existent parts], since that would entail the coexistence of its parts, contrary to its essentially unsettled character. Thus, when a body moving in this way passes from one posited part of a distance to another contiguous part, with no actual part between them, motion occurs without its being intermediate between beginning and end. There is no actual part between them where the moving thing could be situated in the middle, whether one admits a common boundary between them, as the philosophers do, or not, as the theologians do. Appealing here to the possibility of every imagined division, as will follow in—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I259.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I260.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I259.upper.01","volume":1,"page":259,"physical_pdf_page":272,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"إلى آخر متصل به فقد حصلت الحركة وليس هناك توسط بين المبدأ والمنتهى كما حققه بعض المحققين (ولا بد لها) أي للحركة (من) أمور ستة الأول (ما منه) الحركة وهو المبدأ (و) الثاني ما (إليه) الحركة وهو المنتهى (و) الثالث ما (فيه) الحركة وهو مقولة من المقولات الأربع أعني الكم والكيف والأين والوضع (و) الرابع ما (به) أي سببها الفاعلي وهو المحرك فإن الحركة أمر ممكن الوجود فلا بد لها من علة فاعلية (و) الخامس ما (له) الحركة أي محلها لأنها عرض فلا بد لها من محل تقوم به (و) السادس (الزمان) واقتضاء الحركة ما سوى الرابع والخامس","english":"—to another contiguous with it, motion has occurred, yet there is no intermediacy between beginning and end, as certain investigators have established. “Motion requires” six things. First, “that from which” motion proceeds: its beginning. Second, “that toward which” it proceeds: its end. Third, “that in which” it occurs: one of four categories—quantity, quality, where, and position. Fourth, “that by which” it occurs: its efficient cause, the mover. Motion is possible in existence and therefore requires an efficient cause. Fifth, “that to which” it belongs: its subject. Since motion is an accident, it requires a subject in which to inhere. Sixth, “time.” Motion’s requirement of the four things other than the fourth and fifth—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I259.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I258.upper.02","join_out":"I260.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I260.lower.01","volume":1,"page":260,"physical_pdf_page":273,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"المنتهى للحركة الآنية النافي لما عداها لا يجدي في تحقق التوسط المذكور فتفطن فإنه دقيق","english":"—the endpoint, by someone who affirms only instantaneous motion, does not establish the intermediacy in question. Attend to this: it is subtle.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I260.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I259.lower.02","readings":[{"locus":"المنتهى للحركة الآنية النافي لما عداها","status":"The opening compressed phrase is insecure. It is not safe to promote this cross-page sentence to resolved status without a clearer reading."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I260.lower.02","volume":1,"page":260,"physical_pdf_page":273,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله من أجل أنها انتقال تدريجا الخ) فالانتقال لا يوجد بدون المنتقل منه والمنتقل إليه وهما المبدأ والمنتهى والتدريج يقتضي المسافة والزمان لكن ينبغي أن يعلم أن تعلق مطلق الحركة بالزمان بهذا التعلق الذي هو وقوعها فيه غير تعلقها الذي هو حصوله منها فإن الحركة هناك بمنزلة المتبوع لكونها معروضة للزمان وفيما نحن فيه بمنزلة التابع لكونها واقعة فيه كذا قالوا فتدبره","english":"On “Because it is gradual transition, etc.” Transition cannot exist without that from which and that toward which the transition occurs: beginning and end. Gradualness requires distance and time. Yet the dependence of motion in general upon time as something in which motion occurs differs from its relation to time as something arising from motion. In the latter relation motion is what is followed, since time is attributed to it; in the present relation motion follows time, since it occurs in time. Thus they have said. Consider it.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I260.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I260.lower.03","volume":1,"page":260,"physical_pdf_page":273,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله من مكان إلى آخر الخ) إن قيل إذا خرج كل جزء من مكانه فقد خرج الكل إذ الكل ليس إلا مجموع الأجزاء أجيب بأنا لا نسلم أن هناك أجزاء بالفعل وعلى تقدير التسليم فقول ثبوت حكم لكل جزء لا يستلزم ثبوته لمجموع الأجزاء كما سبق والتحقيق أن الحركة الحاصلة للأجزاء الفرضية التي هي حركة أينية لها هي بعينها حركة وضعية بالإضافة إلى الكل فتأمل","english":"On “From one place to another, etc.” If it is said that when every part leaves its place the whole has left its place, since the whole is only the sum of its parts, the reply is that we do not concede actual parts there. Even if they are conceded, what is true of each part need not be true of the aggregate, as stated earlier. The precise account is that the motion belonging to the posited parts, which is local motion for them, is that very same motion considered positionally in relation to the whole. Reflect.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I260.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I260.lower.04","volume":1,"page":260,"physical_pdf_page":273,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله إما بانضمام شيء الخ) أعم من أن يكون من نوع المنضم إليه أولا (قوله بانفصال شيء الخ) أعم من ذلك كذلك","english":"On “Either through the addition of something, etc.”: whether that addition belongs to the same species as the body receiving it or not. On “Through the separation of something, etc.”: likewise, without restriction in that respect.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I260.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I260.lower.05","volume":1,"page":260,"physical_pdf_page":273,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله بما ينضم إليه الخ) أي من نوعه لا بانضمام جسم غريب إليه لأنه قسم آخر من الحركة في الكم كما سيأتي وذلك لأن النمو الحقيقي على ما صرحوا به إنما هو فيما إذا أوردت أجزاء الغذاء في منافذ الأجزاء الأصلية لتغتذي واشتبهت بطبيعة الأصل واندفعت أجزاء الأصل إلى جميع الأقطار على نسبة واحدة في ذلك النوع وزاد مقدار المتغذي","english":"On “Through something joining it, etc.”: something of its own species, not the joining of an alien body, since that is another kind of quantitative motion, as will follow. True growth, as they explicitly state, occurs when particles of nourishment are conveyed into the passages of the original parts to nourish them, become like the original substance in nature, and cause the original parts to extend in all dimensions in a proportion uniform for that species, increasing the magnitude of the nourished organism—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I260.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I261.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I260.upper.01","volume":1,"page":260,"physical_pdf_page":273,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"من الأمور الأربعة من أجل أنها انتقال تدريجا (فالحركة في الأين ظاهرة) لأنها المتبادرة من استعمال أهل اللغة (وفي الوضع كحركة الفلك) فإنه لا يخرج بهذه الحركة من مكان إلى آخر حتى تكون حركته أينية ولكنه يتبدل بها وضعه لأنه يتغير بها نسبة أجزائه إلى أمور خارجة عنه إما مماسة وإما محاذية","english":"—is due to motion’s being gradual transition. “Motion in where is evident,” since it is the meaning immediately understood in ordinary linguistic usage. “Motion in position is exemplified by the motion of the celestial sphere.” In this motion the sphere does not leave one place for another, so its motion is not local. Its position nevertheless changes, because its parts acquire different relations to external things, whether in contact with or facing them.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I260.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I259.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I260.upper.02","volume":1,"page":260,"physical_pdf_page":273,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(وفي الكم) على أربعة أوجه لأن الحركة فيه إما طريق الازدياد أو الانتقاص والأول إما بانضمام شيء أولا والثاني إما بانفصال شيء أولا (كالنمو) وهو ازدياد حجم الجسم بما ينضم إليه ويداخله","english":"“Motion in quantity” has four forms. It proceeds either by increase or decrease. Increase occurs either through the addition of something or without addition; decrease either through the separation of something or without separation. “Growth,” for example, is the increase of a body’s bulk through something joining and entering into it—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I260.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I261.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I261.lower.01","volume":1,"page":261,"physical_pdf_page":274,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"بذلك (قوله في جميع أقطاره الخ) احتراز عن السمن فإن ورود الأجزاء فيه وإن كان على نسبة طبيعية لكن ليس في جميع الأقطار فإنه لا يكون في الطول وفيه نظر يأتي (قوله بنسبة طبيعية الخ) احتراز من الورم بل عن السمن أيضا كما يأتي","english":"—thereby. On “In all its dimensions, etc.” This excludes fattening: although the incoming particles follow a natural proportion, they do not extend all dimensions, since there is no increase in length. A qualification of this will follow. On “In a natural proportion, etc.” This excludes swelling—and also fattening, as will follow.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I261.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I260.lower.05","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I261.lower.02","volume":1,"page":261,"physical_pdf_page":274,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله وهو عكس النمو الخ) فهو انتقاصه بسبب ما ينفصل منه من نوعه فإنه الذبول الحقيقي لا بانفصال جسم غريب لأنه قسم آخر أيضا (قوله في الأقطار بنسبة طبيعية الخ) احتراز من الهزال المقابل للسمن (قوله من غير أن ينضم جسم آخر إليه الخ) أي من غير أن يرد عليه جسم آخر من نوعه وذلك كما في هواء باطن القارورة عند مصها","english":"On “It is the reverse of growth, etc.” It is decrease through the separation of something of the body’s own kind: that is true wasting, not decrease through the separation of an alien body, which is another division. On “Across its dimensions in a natural proportion, etc.” This excludes emaciation, the opposite of fattening. On “Without another body joining it, etc.”: without another body of its kind entering it, as with the air inside a vessel when air is sucked from it.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I261.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I261.lower.03","volume":1,"page":261,"physical_pdf_page":274,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"وتمسكوا في إمكان التخلخل بهذا المعنى الحقيقي بأن الجسم مركب من الهيولى والصورة والهيولى لا صورة لها في نفسها وإنما هي قابلة للمقادير المختلفة بحسب الأسباب المعدة فهي محل محض تتوارد عليه الصور والمقادير من غير أن يقتضي في نفسه شيئا من ذلك فيجوز أن ينتقل من المقدار الصغير إلى الكبير وهو التخلخل وبالعكس وهو التكاثف بالمعنيين الحقيقيين المشهورين","english":"They argue for the possibility of rarefaction in this true sense as follows. Body is composed of prime matter and form. Prime matter has no form in itself but receives different magnitudes according to preparatory causes. It is a bare receptacle upon which forms and magnitudes successively occur, without itself requiring any particular one. A transition from a smaller magnitude to a larger is therefore possible: this is rarefaction. The reverse is condensation, in the familiar proper senses of the terms.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I261.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I261.lower.04","volume":1,"page":261,"physical_pdf_page":274,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله كالماء إذا انجمد الخ) قال في شرح المقاصد ما حاصله إن التخلخل قد يطلق ويراد به تباعد أجزاء الجسم بتداخل جسم غريب كالماء المنجمد إذا ذاب ودخل الهواء بين أجزائه فزاد مقداره بانضمام ما ليس من نوعه وقد يطلق التكاثف ويراد به تقارب الأجزاء بحيث يخرج منها ما بينها من الجسم الغريب كالماء إذا انجمد وخرج الهواء من بين أجزائه فظهر ما في عبارة الشارح «مد ظله» من الاضطراب حيث مثل للتخلخل والتكاثف الحقيقيين بما هو من أفراد هذين المعنيين الأخيرين الغير الحقيقيين وظهر أيضا أن الحركة في مقولة","english":"On “As with water when frozen, etc.” The substance of Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid is that “rarefaction” may mean the separation of a body’s parts through the entry of an alien body—for example, when frozen water melts and air enters between its parts, increasing its magnitude through the addition of something not of its kind. “Condensation” may mean the parts’ drawing together as an alien body between them leaves—for example, when water freezes and air leaves the intervals between its parts. The difficulty in the commentator’s expression—may his protection endure—is therefore evident: he illustrates true rarefaction and condensation with cases belonging to these two latter, non-proper meanings. It is also apparent that motion in the category of—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I261.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I262.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I261.upper.01","volume":1,"page":261,"physical_pdf_page":274,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"في جميع أقطاره بنسبة طبيعية (والذبول) وهو عكس النمو فهو انتقاصه بسبب ما ينفصل عنه في الأقطار بنسبة طبيعية (والتخلخل) وهو ازدياد حجم الجسم من غير أن ينضم جسم آخر إليه كالماء إذا انجمد صغر حجمه بلا انفصال وإذا أذيب عاد إلى حجمه الأول وازداد بلا انضمام (والتكاثف) وهو ضد التخلخل فهو الانتقاص من غير انفصال ومثاله ما مر (وفي الكيف كمسود العنب)","english":"—in all its dimensions in a natural proportion. “Wasting” is the reverse of growth: decrease caused by what separates from the body, across its dimensions in a natural proportion. “Rarefaction” is increase in a body’s bulk without another body joining it. The example given is water: when frozen, its bulk diminishes without anything separating; when melted, it returns to its former bulk and increases without addition. “Condensation” is the opposite of rarefaction: decrease without separation, exemplified by the foregoing case. “Motion in quality is exemplified by the blackening of grapes”—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I261.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I260.upper.02","join_out":"I262.upper.01","editorial":"The water example is translated as the historical text states it, not endorsed as a modern physical description. The lower commentary explicitly challenges the suitability of the example.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I262.lower.01","volume":1,"page":262,"physical_pdf_page":275,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"الكم لا تنحصر في الاعتبارات الأربعة الحقيقية على ما صرح به في شرح المقاصد وأشار إليه هنا بزيادة الكاف في قوله كالنمو الخ فاحفظه فإنه من الدقائق التي لا ينبغي الإمساك منها","english":"—quantity is not restricted to the four proper divisions, as Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid expressly states. Here he indicates this by adding “such as” before “growth, etc.” Remember it: this is a subtle point that should not be overlooked.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I262.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I261.lower.04","readings":[{"locus":"الإمساك منها","status":"The final idiom is uncertain; English gives its apparent admonitory force."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I262.lower.02","volume":1,"page":262,"physical_pdf_page":275,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله وانتقاله من البياض الخ) ويسمى هذا النوع من الحركة استحالة (قوله لأنه لو كانت الأجزاء النارية الخ) وأيضا قالوا إن جبلا من الكبريت إذا اشتعل بنار ولو بقدر يسير فلو كان ذلك بظهور الأجزاء النارية الكامنة لكانت تلك الكامنة أولى بأن تشعل الكبريت ويحس بها وليس كذلك","english":"On “Its transition from whiteness, etc.” This kind of motion is called alteration. On “If fiery particles, etc.” They also argue: if a mountain of sulphur catches fire from even a small flame, and this occurs through the manifestation of hidden fiery particles, those hidden particles would have been even more capable of igniting the sulphur and being perceived. But that is not the case.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I262.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I262.lower.03","volume":1,"page":262,"physical_pdf_page":275,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"ثم اعلم أن المحققين على نفي الحركة في الكم والكيف وكذا على نفي الحركة الوضعية وأنها عائدة إلى الحركة الأينية وبنوا على منعنا قولهم بأنا نجد الجسم ينتقل من كيفية لأخرى ومن كيفية إلى أخرى من غير أن يظهر لنا تفاصيل مراتب الانتقال وأزمنة وجود كل منها فتوهم أن ذلك حركة إذ لا يعقل من الحركة إلا التغير على التدريج لكن عند المحققين لا حركة إذ معنى التدريج في الحركة أن لا تكون دفعة لا بحسب الذات ولا بحسب الأجزاء والانتقال هنا إنما هو دفعات فيتوهم من اجتماعها التدريج لأن ما بين المبدأ والمنتهى من مراتب الكميات أو الكيفيات أمور متغايرة بالفعل ينتقل الجسم من كل منها إلى آخر دفعة فلا تدريج فلا حركة وذلك لأن ما بينهما إن كان واحدا فظاهر أنه لا حركة وإن كان كثيرا فإما أن يكون غير متناه فيلزم المحال من كونه محصورا بين حاصرين أو متناهيا فيلزم تركب الحركة من أمور لا تقبل القسمة فيستلزم الجزء الذي لا يتجزأ وهذا بخلاف الحركة الأينية فإن الوسط الذي بين المبدأ والمنتهى","english":"Know, moreover, that certain investigators deny motion in quantity and quality, and likewise positional motion, reducing the latter to local motion. They reject the assertion: “We find the body passing from one quality to another and then another, without the details of the intermediate stages and their times of existence becoming apparent to us.” This leads one to imagine motion, since motion is understood as gradual change. According to these investigators, however, there is no motion here. Gradualness in motion means that the change occurs neither all at once as a whole nor all at once in its parts. Here transition occurs in discrete occurrences, whose aggregation suggests gradualness. The degrees of quantity or quality between beginning and end are actually distinct, and the body passes from each to another all at once. There is therefore no gradualness and no motion. If there is only one intermediate degree, plainly there is no motion. If there are many, they are either infinite, which entails the impossibility of infinity bounded at both ends, or finite, which entails motion composed of indivisible items and therefore indivisible parts. Local motion is different, because the middle between beginning and end—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I262.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I263.lower.01","readings":[{"locus":"وبنوا على منعنا قولهم","status":"The introductory connective is insecure; the reported objection and its ensuing argument are translated in full."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I262.upper.01","volume":1,"page":262,"physical_pdf_page":275,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"وانتقاله من البياض إلى السواد شيئا فشيئا (وسخن الماء) أي انتقاله من البرودة إلى الحرارة شيئا فشيئا على سبيل التدريج (مع الجزم بعدم الكمون) واستتار الأجزاء النارية (فيه) تبرد لأسباب خارجة عنه على ما زعم البعض وذلك لأنه لو كانت الأجزاء النارية كامنة في الماء البارد لوجب أن يحس بحرها من أدخل يده فيه وليس كذلك بل ربما يجمد باطنه أبرد من ظاهره (أو الورود) أي ورود أجزاء نارية من الخارج (عليه) على ما زعم البعض الآخر وفساده ظاهر","english":"—its transition little by little from whiteness to blackness—and “the warming of water,” its gradual transition from coldness to heat, “with certainty that there is no latency”: no fiery particles concealed “within it,” becoming cold through external causes, as some claim. If fiery particles lay hidden in cold water, someone placing a hand in it would have to feel their heat; this is not so. Indeed, its interior may be found colder than its surface. “Nor is there an influx,” that is, fiery particles arriving “from outside,” as others claim. The falsity of that account is evident.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I262.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I261.upper.01","readings":[{"locus":"يجمد باطنه","status":"Probably يجد باطنه (“finds its interior”), not “it freezes”; English follows the comparison colder than its surface. To be resolved in proof."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I263.lower.01","volume":1,"page":263,"physical_pdf_page":276,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"واحد بالفعل يقبل بحسب الفرض انقسامات غير متناهية فلا يلزم تركيب الحركة مما لا يقبل الانقسام ورد بأن التقدير هو أن الانتقال إلى كل من الآحاد دفعي فلا فرق في ذلك بين الأينية وغيرها كذا حققه بعضهم (أقول) إذا تقرر أن الانتقال الدفعي ليس بحركة لاعتبار التدريج فيها فكيف يصح جعل ما هو بمعنى التوسط الذي هو انتقال دفعي قسما من الحركة قلت لم يجعلوه قسما من الحركة التدريجية لأن الظاهر أن لفظ الحركة مشترك لفظي بينهما كما يدل عليه عبارة المصنف في المقاصد حيث قال لفظ الحركة يطلق على معنيين أحدهما الخ وثانيهما الخ لكن فيه تأمل فليتأمل وسيأتي ما له تعلق بهذا","english":"—is actually one and admits infinitely many hypothetical divisions. Motion need not, therefore, be composed of indivisibles. The objection is that, on the hypothesis in question, transition to each individual position is instantaneous; in this respect there is no difference between local motion and the others. Thus some investigators explain it. I say: if an instantaneous transition is not motion because motion requires gradualness, how can motion as intermediacy, which is an instantaneous transition, be a division of motion? I reply: they did not make it a division of gradual motion. Apparently the word “motion” is equivocal between the two senses, as the author’s wording in al-Maqāṣid suggests: “The term motion is used in two senses: the first … and the second ….” Nevertheless, the matter merits consideration. A related point will follow.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I263.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I262.lower.03","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I263.lower.02","volume":1,"page":263,"physical_pdf_page":276,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله أي بلا واسطة عروضها الخ) أي المنفي في الحركة بالذات هو الواسطة في العروض لا في الثبوت فإنها موجودة في الحركة بالذات وبالعرض كليهما (قوله إذ لا تبدل لسطح السفينة الخ) هذا مبني على كون المكان هو السطح الباطن الخ (قوله لم تم الخ) إشارة إلى منع كون المكان هو السطح (قوله الحالة المستمرة الخ) وهي الكون المتوسط أو كيفية بها يكون التوسط للجسم على ما مر وإنما لم يكتف","english":"On “Without its belonging through another thing, etc.” What is denied in intrinsic motion is mediation in attribution, not mediation in establishment: the latter exists in both intrinsic and accidental motion. On “Since the ship’s surface does not change, etc.” This presupposes that place is the inner surface [of the containing body]. On the subsequent objection: it indicates a denial that place is surface. On “The continuing condition, etc.” This is the state of intermediacy, or the quality through which the body is intermediate, as above. He does not simply content himself—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I263.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I264.lower.01","readings":[{"locus":"قوله لم تم","status":"The short quoted lemma is illegible here; its referent is the immediately preceding objection."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I263.ruled.01","volume":1,"page":263,"physical_pdf_page":276,"layer":"ruled","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله وتكون بالذات) واعلم أن بالذات يستعمل بمعنى بالحقيقة المقابل بالمجاز وبمعنى أولا وبلا واسطة كما فسرناه به أولا والتوجيه الأول مبني على المعنى الأول والثاني على الثاني فافهم اه منه مد ظله","english":"On “It may belong intrinsically.” Know that “intrinsically” is used both for “in reality,” as opposed to figuratively, and for “primarily and without mediation,” as we first explained it. The first explanation rests on the first sense, the second on the second. Understand. End. [Signed:] “From him, may his protection endure.”","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I263.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I263.upper.01","volume":1,"page":263,"physical_pdf_page":276,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(وتكون الحركة بالذات) أي بلا واسطة عروضها لشيء آخر (كحركة السفينة و) تكون أيضا (بالعرض كحركة راكبها) لأن الحركة هي الانتقال من مكان إلى مكان آخر مغاير للأول بجميع أجزائه ومكان الراكب ليس متبدلا بجميع أجزائه إذ لا تبدل لسطح السفينة وإن تبدل الهواء فليس لها وجود مع الراكب وإنما يوصف بها تبعا للسفينة","english":"“Motion may belong intrinsically,” that is, without its belonging through another thing, “as with the motion of a ship”; or “accidentally, as with the motion of its passenger.” Motion is transition from one place to another differing from the first in all its parts. The passenger’s place is not changed in every part, since the ship’s surface does not change, even if the air changes. Motion therefore does not belong to the passenger himself; he is described as moving in dependence upon the ship.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I263.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I263.upper.02","volume":1,"page":263,"physical_pdf_page":276,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"ولا يخفى أن هذا الوجه لم يدل على أنها لا توجد للسفينة حينئذ حتى يوصف الراكب بها تبعا لها ضرورة أن مكانها أيضا لم يتبدل بجميع أجزائه لبقاء السطح الذي يلاقى بها من الراكب فالحق أن الحركة هي الحالة المستمرة من أول المسافة","english":"It is evident, however, that this explanation does not establish motion’s belonging to the ship in that case, so that the passenger could be described as moving through it: the ship’s place also has not changed in all its parts, since the surface of the passenger in contact with it remains. The true account is that motion is the condition continuing from the beginning of the distance—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I263.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I264.upper.01","readings":[{"locus":"لم يدل على أنها لا توجد للسفينة","status":"The clustered words concerning the failure of the argument are insecure. English follows the explicit objection that the same criterion excludes the ship’s own motion; no secure diplomatic amendment is claimed."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I264.lower.01","volume":1,"page":264,"physical_pdf_page":277,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"في الجواب بكون المكان هو البعد بل أجاب بتحقيق معنى الحركة ليتم الجواب على المذهبين في المكان (قوله إلا أن عروضها له الخ) لا خفاء في كون حركة جالسها بالعرض لكن قد يناقش في كون حركة السفينة بالذات بل هي أيضا تتبع حركة المياه والرياح إلا أن يقال إن كونها بالذات إنما هو بالإضافة إلى حركة جالسها مع أنه مناقشة في المثال","english":"—in replying, with the statement that place is extension. Instead he establishes the meaning of motion so that the answer works on both theories of place. On “Except that it belongs to him, etc.” The seated passenger’s motion is plainly accidental. One may nevertheless question whether the ship’s motion is intrinsic, since it too follows the movement of water and wind. It may be replied that its intrinsic character is only relative to the passenger’s motion. In any case, this is an objection to the example.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I264.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I263.lower.02","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I264.lower.02","volume":1,"page":264,"physical_pdf_page":277,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله أو متعلقا به الخ) أي التعلق المخصوص لا مطلق التعلق فيكون قوله كتعلق النفس الخ قيدا لما قبله لا محض التمثيل فإن قيل فعلى رأي من جعل الممكنات كلها مستندة إلى الله تعالى ابتداء هل يتأتى هذا التقسيم أم تكون المحركات كلها قسرية قلنا بل يتأتى بأن يراد بالمحرك ما جرت عادة الله تعالى بخلق الحركة معه وبقالة المحرك إما أن يكون خارجا الخ","english":"On “Or connected with it, etc.” This means the specific kind of connection, not connection without qualification. “As the soul is connected, etc.” therefore restricts the preceding statement rather than merely furnishing an example. An objection: on the view that all contingent things depend immediately upon God, does this division hold, or must all motions be constrained? We reply that it holds if “mover” means that with which God customarily creates motion. One may then say: the mover is either external, etc.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I264.jpg","stage":69,"readings":[{"locus":"وبقالة المحرك","status":"The words before المحرك are unclear; English renders the evident resumption of the division."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I264.lower.03","volume":1,"page":264,"physical_pdf_page":277,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله أي شعور مبدأ الحركة الخ) إن قيل كما أن المحرك الغير الخارج يكون تارة مع القصد والشعور وتارة بدونهما كذلك المحرك الخارج فلم لم يجعلوا الحركة الصادرة من الخارج على قسمين كما جعلوا الصادرة من غير الخارج كذلك قلت الاختلاف بالشعور وعدمه إنما يفيد اختلاف الحركة إذا كان في المتحرك دون المحرك فقط والمحرك الغير الخارج لما كان جزءا من المتحرك أو كالجزء منه يكون اختلافه بالشعور وعدمه موجبا لاختلاف حركة المتحرك بخلاف المحرك الخارج فإن حركته","english":"On “Awareness of the motion by its principle, etc.” An objection: just as a non-external mover may act with or without intention and awareness, so may an external mover. Why, then, is externally caused motion not divided in two, as internally caused motion is? I reply: the distinction between awareness and its absence differentiates the motion only when it belongs to the moving thing, not merely to its mover. Since the non-external mover is a part of the moving thing, or like a part, its having or lacking awareness differentiates the moving thing’s motion. This is not so with an external mover: the moving thing’s motion—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I264.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I265.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I264.upper.01","volume":1,"page":264,"physical_pdf_page":277,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"إلى آخرها وهي غير استبدال المكان وهي عارضة للراكب كالسفينة إلا أن عروضها له بواسطة عروضها لها (والمحرك إن كان خارجا) عن المتحرك (فحركته قسرية وإلا) يكن خارجا بأن كان جزءا منه أو متعلقا به كتعلق النفس بالبدن (فمع القصد والشعور) أي شعور مبدأ الحركة بتلك الحركة (إرادية وبدونهما) أي القصد والشعور (طبيعية فيدخل فيها) أي الطبيعية (حركة النمو) إذ النامي يقتضي الزيادة في الأقطار عند ورود الغذاء بلا قصد وشعور (و) حركة (النبض) لأنها ليست بحسب القصد ولا بقاسر في الخارج بل بما في القلب من القوة الحيوانية","english":"—to its end. It is something other than replacing one place with another, and belongs to the passenger as to the ship, except that it belongs to the passenger through belonging to the ship. “If the mover is external” to the moving thing, “its motion is constrained. Otherwise”—if the mover is a part of it or connected with it as the soul is connected with the body—“with intention and awareness,” meaning awareness of the motion by its principle, the motion “is voluntary. Without them,” intention and awareness, “it is natural.” Thus natural motion includes “growth”: the growing thing requires increase in dimensions on receiving nourishment without intention or awareness. It also includes “the pulse,” since pulsation is neither intentional nor caused by an external constrainer, but arises from the vital power in the heart.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I264.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I263.upper.02","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I264.upper.02","volume":1,"page":264,"physical_pdf_page":277,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(و) كذا يدخل فيها (حركة النفس) بفتح الفاء (من حيث الاحتياج إلى مطلقها) أي مطلق حركة النفس (وأما من حيث إمكان تغيير جزئياتها عن أوقاتها) التي وقوعها","english":"It also includes “the movement of breath”—nafas, with fatḥa on the fāʾ—“insofar as there is a need for it without qualification,” that is, for breathing in general. “But insofar as particular instances can be shifted from their times,” the times at which they occur—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I264.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I265.upper.01","editorial":"النفس here explicitly means breath, not soul. The source itself specifies the vocalisation.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I265.lower.01","volume":1,"page":265,"physical_pdf_page":278,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"مقسورة لا تختلف باختلافه بالشعور وعدمه لمغايرة المحرك والمتحرك بالكلية حينئذ فتدبره واحفظه","english":"—is constrained and does not differ with the external mover’s awareness or lack of awareness, since mover and moving thing are then wholly distinct. Consider and remember this.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I265.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I264.lower.03","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I265.lower.02","volume":1,"page":265,"physical_pdf_page":278,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله يتمكن المتنفس من أن يقدمها الخ) وما يقال من أن النائم لا إرادة له فيلزم أن لا يتنفس ليس بشيء لأن النائم يفعل الحركات الإرادية وإن لم يشعر بالإرادة أو لم يتذكر الشعور ولذا فيه عمل الأعضاء عند الحاجة إلى الحك مع عدم شعوره بذلك","english":"On “The person breathing can advance it, etc.” The objection that a sleeping person has no will and therefore should not breathe is worthless. A sleeping person performs voluntary movements even without being aware of the volition, or without remembering that awareness. Thus his limbs act when there is a need to scratch, although he is unaware of it.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I265.jpg","stage":69,"readings":[{"locus":"ولذا فيه عمل الأعضاء","status":"The short connecting phrase is unclear; the limb movement and scratching example are secure."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I265.lower.03","volume":1,"page":265,"physical_pdf_page":278,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله فقد تفعل حركات الخ) قد يقال إن البسائط تفعل حركات إلى جهات كصعود الماء إذا وقع تحت الأرض وهبوطه إذا وقع فوق الهواء فهي لا تكون حركتها إلا على نهج واحد أعني صوب الحيز فافهم","english":"On “May produce motions, etc.” It may be said that simple bodies too produce motions in different directions: water rises when below the earth and falls when above air. Their motion nevertheless follows only one pattern, namely toward their natural spatial region. Understand.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I265.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I265.lower.04","volume":1,"page":265,"physical_pdf_page":278,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله ثلاثة منها الخ) فإنهم اتفقوا على أن تعلق الحركة بهذه الثلاثة بمنزلة العرضي بخلاف تعلقها بالثلاثة الباقية أعني ما منه وما إليه وما فيه فإن تعلقها بها بمنزلة الذاتي فتختلف ماهية الحركة باختلافها (قوله لأنه إذا اختلف الخ) أي ما فيه وإنما المراد بوحدته وكذا وحدة المبدأ والمنتهى هو الوحدة النوعية كما صرح به","english":"On “Three of them, etc.” They agree that motion’s dependence on those three is analogous to an accidental relation. Its dependence on the other three—beginning, end and category—is analogous to an essential relation, so its quiddity differs with their difference. On “Because, if it differs, etc.”: that in which motion occurs. Its unity, like that of beginning and end, means specific unity, as he explicitly states.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I265.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I265.lower.05","volume":1,"page":265,"physical_pdf_page":278,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله كالحركة الأينية والكيفية الخ) فيه نظر فإن التمثيل لاختلاف ما فيه بما ذكر يفيد بظاهره أنه لو اتحد المبدأ والمنتهى","english":"On “As with local and qualitative motion, etc.” This is questionable. Illustrating difference in the category by those examples apparently implies that, if beginning and end were identical—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I265.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I266.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I265.upper.01","volume":1,"page":265,"physical_pdf_page":278,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"فيها على مجراها الطبيعي (فإرادية) يتمكن المتنفس من أن يقدمها على أوقاتها وأن يؤخرها عنها بحسب إرادته (وما قيل إن) الحركة (الطبيعية لا تكون إلا هابطة أو صاعدة) فلا تكون مثل حركة النبض الطبيعية مدفوع بأن ذلك (إنما هو في البسائط العنصرية وأما الطبيعة الحيوانية أو النباتية فقد تفعل حركات إلى جهات وغايات مختلفة)","english":"—in their natural course, breathing “is voluntary”: the person breathing can advance or delay particular breaths according to his will. “As for the assertion that natural motion is only downward or upward,” which would exclude a natural motion such as the pulse, the answer is that this applies “only to simple elemental bodies. Animal or vegetative nature may produce motions toward different directions and ends.”","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I265.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I264.upper.02","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I265.upper.02","volume":1,"page":265,"physical_pdf_page":278,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"ثم اعلم أن اختلاف الحركات قد يكون بالماهية وقد يكون بالعوارض واتحادها قد يكون بالشخص وقد يكون بالنوع وقد يكون بالجنس ثم قد توصف بالتضاد وقد توصف بالانقسام (و) قد مر أن الحركة مفتقرة إلى أمور ستة فاقتصروا على أن ماهية الحركة لا تختلف باختلاف ثلاثة منها ما به ولها والزمان بل باختلاف ما به لا تختلف هو بها أيضا فإذن (وحدتها النوعية بوحدة ما فيه) لأنه إذا اختلف اختلفت الحركة كالحركة الأينية والكيفية (و) بوحدة (ما منه وما إليه)","english":"Know, moreover, that motions may differ in quiddity or in accidental features. Their unity may be numerical, specific or generic; they may also be described as contrary or divisible. Motion was previously said to require six things. They restrict the account by saying that its quiddity does not differ with three of them: that by which it occurs, that to which it belongs, and time. Indeed, differences in its mover do not differentiate motion [in kind], nor is the mover differentiated by such differences in motion. Hence “its specific unity is through unity of that in which it occurs”: difference in that category differentiates motion, as with local and qualitative motion; “and” through unity of “that from which and that toward which it proceeds.”","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I265.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I266.upper.01","readings":[{"locus":"بل باختلاف ما به لا تختلف هو بها أيضا","status":"The compressed reciprocity clause is grammatically insecure in the scan. The six-factor division itself is clear."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I266.lower.01","volume":1,"page":266,"physical_pdf_page":279,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"واتحد مطلق الحركة الأينية مثلا كانت واحدة بالنوع وليس كذلك فإنهم قالوا إذا اختلف ما فيه الحركة كانت مختلفة بالنوع وإن اتحد المبدأ والمنتهى ومثلوا لذلك بالحركة من نقطة إلى أخرى على الاستقامة مع الحركة منها إليها مع الانحناء مع أن ما فيه الحركتين متحد في مطلق الأينية فالأولى التمثيل له بهذا أو بالحركة من البياض إلى السواد على طريق الأخذ في الصفرة ثم الحمرة ثم السواد مع الحركة منه إليه على طريق الأخذ في الخضرة ثم النيلية ثم السواد فتدبر","english":"—and local motion in general were the same, the motion would be one in species. That is not so. They say that when the path or category through which motion occurs differs, motion differs in species even if beginning and end are the same. Their example is motion between two points along a straight path as against motion between the same points along a curve, although both fall under local motion. This would be a better example; or transition from white to black through yellow, then red, then black, compared with the same transition through green, then indigo, then black. Reflect.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","source_image":"source_images/I266.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I265.lower.05","proof_notes":["Whole upper and lower registers reread in magnified source crops; the false lower continuation to I267 removed, actual upper continuation retained."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I266.lower.02","volume":1,"page":266,"physical_pdf_page":279,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله إذا اختلف المبدأ والمنتهى الخ) يعني وإن اتحد ما فيه (قوله كالصاعدة والهابطة الخ) فإن طرفيهما وإن لم يختلفا بالماهية لكنهما اختلفا بالمبدئية والنهاية وهما متقابلان وهذا المقدار كاف في وقوع الاختلاف في نوع الحركتين إن قيل هذا جار في كل حركة من مبدأ إلى منتهى مع رجوع عنه قلت لما كان مبدأ الصعود والهبوط ومنتهاهما في جهتين حقيقيتين غير متبدلين اعتبر الاختلاف في ذلك دون سائر الجهات فتدبر","english":"On “If beginning and end differ, etc.”: even if that in which motion occurs is the same. On “As with ascent and descent, etc.” Their two termini may not differ in quiddity, but they differ as beginning and end, two opposed roles. That suffices for a difference in species between the motions. If it is objected that the same applies to every motion from beginning to end followed by return, I reply: the beginning and end of ascent and descent occupy two real, fixed directions. Difference was therefore taken into account here, not in all other directions. Reflect.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","source_image":"source_images/I266.jpg","stage":69,"proof_notes":["Whole upper and lower registers reread in magnified source crops; the false lower continuation to I267 removed, actual upper continuation retained."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I266.lower.03","volume":1,"page":266,"physical_pdf_page":279,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله فإن تنوع المحل لا يوجب الخ) ولهذا يظهر أن لا أثر للاختلاف بالقسر والطبع والإرادة فالحركة الصاعدة للنار طبعا وللحجر قسرا وللطير إرادة لا تختلف نوعا (قوله لا يتصور فيه اختلاف الماهية الخ) ولو فرض ذلك فلا خفاء في جواز إحاطتها بحقيقة واحدة","english":"On “A difference in the subject’s species does not require, etc.” This also shows that the difference between constraint, nature and will has no effect [upon specific unity]: upward motion of fire naturally, of a stone by constraint, and of a bird voluntarily do not differ in species. On “It admits no difference in quiddity, etc.” Even supposing such a difference, it is plainly possible for them to encompass one and the same reality.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","source_image":"source_images/I266.jpg","stage":69,"proof_notes":["Lower enlargement resolves إحاطتها بحقيقة واحدة. The sentence is complete on I266; the previous cross-page join was spurious.","Whole upper and lower registers reread in magnified source crops; the false lower continuation to I267 removed, actual upper continuation retained."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I266.upper.01","volume":1,"page":266,"physical_pdf_page":279,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"لأنه إذا اختلف المبدأ أو المنتهى اختلفت كالصاعدة والهابطة بخلاف ما له الحركة فإن تنوع المحل لا يوجب تنوع الحال فتسود العنب والإنسان من نوع وبخلاف الزمان لأنه نوع واحد لا يتصور فيه اختلاف بالماهية وكذا ما به الحركة فإن اختلافه لا يوجب اختلاف الشخص فالنوع أولى","english":"—because differences in beginning or end differentiate motions, as with ascent and descent. This is not so with the subject of motion: a difference in the subject’s species does not require a difference in the species of what inheres in it. The blackening of grapes and of a human being is one species. Nor does time differentiate motion in species, since time is one species and admits no difference of quiddity. Likewise, difference of mover does not even require numerical difference of motion, still less specific difference.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","source_image":"source_images/I266.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I265.upper.02","proof_notes":["Upper enlargement resolves فتسود العنب, “thus the blackening of grapes,” not قسود.","Whole upper and lower registers reread in magnified source crops; the false lower continuation to I267 removed, actual upper continuation retained."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I266.upper.02","volume":1,"page":266,"physical_pdf_page":279,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(و) وحدتها (الشخصية بوحدة ما سوى المحرك) من الأمور الخمسة للقطع بأن حركة زيد غير حركة عمرو لأن العرض الواحد بالشخص لا يقوم بمحلين وأن حركة زيد اليوم غير حركته أمس لعدم استقرار الزمان وحركته في هذا الموضع غيرها في آخر وحركته من نقطة إلى نقطة غير حركته بالعكس وكذا في الكم والكيف والوضع وأما وحدة المحرك فلا عبرة بها في وحدة الحركة لأن الحركة الواحدة التي لا تكثر فيها بالفعل قد تقع بمؤثرات كثيرة كحركة الجسم في مسافة بتلاحق المحركات وحركة","english":"“Its numerical unity is through unity of the five things other than the mover.” Zayd’s motion is certainly different from ʿAmr’s, since one numerically identical accident cannot inhere in two subjects. Zayd’s motion today differs from his motion yesterday, since time does not remain fixed. His motion in this location differs from that in another; motion from one point to another differs from motion in the reverse direction. The same applies in quantity, quality and position. The mover’s unity is irrelevant to motion’s unity: one motion with no actual multiplicity may be produced by several successive agents, as when a body crosses a distance with movers acting successively; and the motion of—","reading_status":"second_visual_read","source_image":"source_images/I266.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I267.upper.01","proof_notes":["Upper image confirms كحركة الجسم ... بتلاحق المحركات. The motion of water continues onto I267.","Whole upper and lower registers reread in magnified source crops; the false lower continuation to I267 removed, actual upper continuation retained."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I267.lower.01","volume":1,"page":267,"physical_pdf_page":280,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله لا يقدح في وحدتها الخ) قد يقال إن أريد بتلك الحركة معنى القطع أعني الامتداد الموهوم فلا وجود لها في الخارج أو معنى الكون في الوسط أعني الحالة المستمرة الغير المستقرة فهو كلي والواقع بهذا المحرك جزئي مغاير للواقع بذلك فلا تتصور حركة واحدة بالشخص واقعة بمحركين وأجيب بأن الظاهر هو الأول ومعنى كونه وهما هو أنه بصفة الامتداد والاجتماع لا يوجد إلا في الوهم وإلا فأبعاضها موجودة في الخارج غايته أنه على سبيل التجدد كما مر","english":"On “Does not impair its unity, etc.” One might say: if this motion means traversal—the imagined extension—it has no extramental existence. If it means being intermediate—the enduring but nonstationary condition—it is a universal, and the particular brought about by this mover differs from the particular brought about by that mover. A numerically single motion brought about by two movers is therefore inconceivable. The reply is that the first meaning is apparently intended. Its being imagined means that, under the qualification of extension and simultaneous collection, it exists only in imagination. Otherwise, its parts do exist outside the mind, albeit successively renewed, as explained earlier.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I267.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I267.lower.02","volume":1,"page":267,"physical_pdf_page":280,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله وحدة جنسية الخ) حتى إن الحركة في الكم والكيف والأين والوضع أجناس مختلفة وحركة النمو والذبول والتخلخل والتكاثف جنس واحد وبهذا اتضح حديث النظر الذي سبق في التمثيل المذكور للشارح «مد ظله» فتذكره","english":"On “A generic unity, etc.” Accordingly, motions in quantity, quality, place and position belong to different genera; whereas growth, wasting, rarefaction and condensation form one genus. This clarifies the earlier reservation about the commentator’s example—may his protection endure. Recall it.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I267.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I267.lower.03","volume":1,"page":267,"physical_pdf_page":280,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله إذا لم يكن مطلق الحركة جنسا الخ) قد يقال لو كانت الحركة جنسا لأقسامها لزادت المقولات على العشرة لأنها حينئذ تكون جنسا عاليا فافهم","english":"On “If motion taken absolutely is not a genus, etc.” One might say: if motion were a genus of its divisions, the categories would exceed ten, for motion would then be a highest genus. Understand this.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I267.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I267.lower.04","volume":1,"page":267,"physical_pdf_page":280,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله لأن الصاعدة والهابطة متضادتان الخ) قد مر أن اختلاف","english":"On “Because ascent and descent are contrary, etc.” It was explained earlier that the difference—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I267.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I268.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I267.upper.01","volume":1,"page":267,"physical_pdf_page":280,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"الماء في الحرارة بتلاحق النيران ولا يلزم من ذلك اجتماع المؤثرين على أثر واحد لأن تأثير كل إنما يكون في أمر آخر هو بمنزلة البعض من الحركة وهذا البعض لا يقدح في وحدتها على الاتصال لأنه بمجرد الوهم من غير انقسام بالفعل","english":"—water in respect of heat, through successive fires. This does not entail that two efficient causes converge upon a single effect: each acts upon something different, which is as a part of the motion. Such a part does not impair the motion’s continuous unity, since it is distinguished merely in thought, without an actual division.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I267.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I266.upper.02","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I267.upper.02","volume":1,"page":267,"physical_pdf_page":280,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(و) وحدتها (الجنسية بوحدة ما فيه) من وحدة جنسية واعترض بأنه إنما يصح إذا لم يكن مطلق الحركة جنسا لما تحته بل يكون مقولية الحركة على الحركة الكمية والكيفية والأينية والوضعية بالاشتراك اللفظي حتى لا تكون أعم أو بالتشكيك حتى تكون عرضا للأقسام","english":"“And” its “generic unity is through the unity of that in which” it occurs—a generic unity. It has been objected that this is correct only if motion taken absolutely is not a genus of what falls under it. Rather, motion would have to be predicated of quantitative, qualitative, local and positional motion either equivocally, so that it would not be a more general [common nature], or by graded predication, so that it would be accidental to those divisions.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I267.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I267.upper.03","volume":1,"page":267,"physical_pdf_page":280,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(وتضادها بتضاد ما منه وما إليه) ولا عبرة بتضاد المحرك لتضاد الحركتين مع اتحاد المحرك كما في حركة الجسم صعودا وهبوطا بالإرادة وبالقسر وتماثلهما مع تضاد المحركين كما في الحركة الصاعدة للحجر والنار بالقسر والطبيعة ولا بتضاد المتحرك لأن حركة الحجر قسرا إلى فوق وطبعا إلى تحت متضادتان مع اتحاد المتحرك ولا بتضاد ما فيه لأن الصاعدة والهابطة متضادتان مع اتحاد ما فيه","english":"“Its contrariety is through the contrariety of that from which and that to which” it proceeds. The contrariety of the mover is not decisive: two motions may be contrary while their mover is the same, as when a body moves upwards and downwards through volition or constraint; conversely, motions may be alike although their movers are contrary, as in the upward motion of a stone through constraint and of fire by nature. Nor is the contrariety of the moving subject decisive, since a stone’s forced upward motion and natural downward motion are contrary although the moving subject is the same. Nor is the contrariety of the category in which motion occurs decisive: ascent and descent are contrary, yet occur in the same category—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I267.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I268.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I268.lower.01","volume":1,"page":268,"physical_pdf_page":281,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"طرفي الحركة في هذه الصورة إنما هو بعارض المبدئية أي القرب إلى المحيط والبعد من المركز أو بالعكس والنهاية كذلك فعند القول بتضاد المبدأ والمنتهى في هذه الصورة دون ما فيه تحكم إذ الأيون المتوسطة القرب والبعد المذكورين أيضا فيها أن لا تكون تلك الغاية من القرب والبعد (أقول) ليس المبدئية والنهاية عبارتين عن القرب والبعد المذكورين فقط بل مع اعتبار ابتداء الحركة وانتهائها والأيون المتوسطة من حيث إنها متوسطة لم يعتبر فيها الابتداء والانتهاء وإن لم تحصل فيهما القرب والبعد فباعتبار المبدأ والانتهاء اندفع الإيراد بالأيون المتوسطة وباعتبار القرب والبعد المذكورين اندفع الإيراد بالمبدأ والمنتهى وسائر الجهات الغير الحقيقية كما مر فتدبر","english":"—between the two termini of motion in this case arises only through the circumstance of being a starting point—namely, nearness to the circumference and distance from the centre, or the reverse—and similarly for the endpoint. To affirm contrariety of the starting point and endpoint here, but not of that in which the motion occurs, is therefore arbitrary: intermediate positions likewise possess the mentioned nearness and distance, although not to the same extreme degree. I say: being a starting point or an endpoint does not mean that nearness or distance alone, but includes consideration of the motion’s beginning and ending. Intermediate positions, precisely as intermediate, are not considered in respect of beginning and ending, [the connecting clause concerning their nearness and distance is insecure]. Considering the beginning and ending removes the objection based on intermediate positions; considering the specified nearness and distance removes the objection based on starting points, endpoints and other non-genuine directions, as explained earlier. Reflect.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I268.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I267.lower.04","readings":[{"locus":"الأيون المتوسطة ... وإن لم تحصل فيهما القرب والبعد","status":"Several connectors are indistinct. The argumentative structure is rendered, but this short clause remains unresolved and is not counted as gap-free."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I268.lower.02","volume":1,"page":268,"physical_pdf_page":281,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله وهو ظاهر الخ) وأما عدم انقسامها بانقسام الثلاثة الباقية فهو وإن ذلك ظاهر في المبدأ والمنتهى وأما في المحرك ففيه تفصيل وهو أنه قد ينقسم وقد لا ينقسم وعلى تقدير الانقسام قد يقوى البعض على التحريك وقد لا يقوى","english":"On “This is evident, etc.” As for its not being divided through division of the remaining three [requisites], this is evident in the case of the starting point and endpoint. The mover, however, requires distinction: it may or may not be divisible; and, when divisible, one of its parts may or may not be capable of moving [the subject].","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I268.jpg","stage":69,"readings":[{"locus":"فهو وإن ذلك","status":"The short introductory connector is insecure; the three-way classification and ensuing distinction are clear."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I268.lower.03","volume":1,"page":268,"physical_pdf_page":281,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله بالشدة والضعف الخ) ينبغي أن يعلم أنه هل ينتهيان إلى حد حتى تتحقق حركة سريعة لا حظ لها من البطء وبطيئة لا حظ لها من السرعة أو لا بل لكل حركة حظ من السرعة بالنسبة إلى ما هو أبطأ ومن البطء بالنسبة إلى ما هو أسرع فيه تردد والأشبه بأصولهم هو الثاني","english":"On “In intensity and weakness, etc.” One should determine whether the two reach a limit, so that there could be a fast motion with no share of slowness and a slow motion with no share of speed; or whether, instead, every motion has a share of speed relative to what is slower and a share of slowness relative to what is faster. There is room for hesitation. The second alternative is more consistent with their principles.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I268.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I268.upper.01","volume":1,"page":268,"physical_pdf_page":281,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"ولا بتضاد الزمان لأنه ليس فيه اختلاف ماهية فضلا عن التضاد ثم التضاد قد يكون بالذات (كالتسود) أي الحركة من البياض إلى السواد (والتبيض) بالعكس (و) قد يكون بالعرض (كالصعود) أي الحركة من الحضيض إلى الأوج (والهبوط) عكس الصعود فإن مبدأيهما من التضاد بعارض كون أحدهما في القرب من المركز والبعد من المحيط والآخر بالعكس وكذا المنتهى فليس بينهما تضاد بالذات لأنهما جزءان من جسم عرض لهما التضاد بالقرب والبعد مع التساوي بالحقيقة","english":"—nor is contrariety of time decisive, since time contains no difference of quiddity, much less contrariety. Contrariety may be intrinsic, “as in becoming black”—motion from whiteness to blackness—“and becoming white,” the reverse. “And” it may be accidental, “as in ascent”—motion from the lowest point to the highest—“and descent,” the reverse of ascent. The contrariety of their starting points arises through the incidental circumstance that one is near the centre and far from the circumference, while the other is the reverse; likewise their endpoints. They are not intrinsically contrary: they are two portions of a body, equal in nature, to which contrariety accrues through nearness and distance.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I268.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I267.upper.03","readings":[{"locus":"فإن مبدأيهما من التضاد بعارض","status":"The clause around من التضاد is grammatically compressed in this scan; its evident contrast is intrinsic versus accidental contrariety. Retained without silent emendation."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I268.upper.02","volume":1,"page":268,"physical_pdf_page":281,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(وانقسامها بانقسام الزمان) وهو ظاهر فإن الحركة في نصف ساعة نصف الحركة في ساعة (و) بانقسام (ما فيه) فإن الحركة إلى نصف مسافة نصف الحركة في كلها (و) بانقسام (ما له) لأن ما يقوم بجزء من المتحرك غير ما يقوم بالجزء الآخر (ومن لوازم الحركة كيفية مقارنة) بالشدة والضعف","english":"“Its divisibility follows the divisibility of time.” This is evident: the motion in half an hour is half the motion in an hour. “And” it follows the divisibility of “that in which” motion occurs: motion over half a distance is half the motion over the whole. “And” it follows the divisibility of “that to which it belongs,” since what inheres in one part of the moving subject differs from what inheres in another part. “Among motion’s concomitants is a quality admitting comparison” in intensity and weakness—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I268.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I269.upper.01","editorial":"The fractions concern corresponding parts of the motion under discussion; no unrestricted claim about unequal velocities is inserted.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I269.lower.01","volume":1,"page":269,"physical_pdf_page":282,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"وإن مال الإمام إلى الأول تمسكا بما لا يغني ضعفه تفسير (قوله لامتناع أن يكون الشيء مقتضيا لأمر ومانعا منه الخ) اقتضاء بالطبع وأما إذا كان بالإرادة فلا امتناع من الاقتضاء والمنع فتدبر","english":"—although the Imām inclined to the first alternative, relying on something whose weakness needs no exposition. On “Since a thing cannot both necessitate something and impede it, etc.” This means necessitating by nature. Where it is through volition, there is no impossibility in both necessitating and preventing. Reflect.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I269.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I268.lower.03","readings":[{"locus":"بما لا يغني ضعفه تفسير","status":"The short expression about the weakness of the argument is indistinct; the translation gives its apparent sense, not a secure diplomatic reading."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I269.lower.02","volume":1,"page":269,"physical_pdf_page":282,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله مع خلوص المقتضي وعدم رفع الخ) قضية المتن هنا","english":"On “Together with the unimpeded necessitating cause and the failure to remove, etc.” The proposition in the base text here is—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I269.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I270.lower_continuation.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I269.ruled.01","volume":1,"page":269,"physical_pdf_page":282,"layer":"ruled","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله مع خلوص المقتضي لها) وإنما قال مع خلوص المقتضي دون وجوده للإشارة إلى أن الحركة لا تقع بدون خلوص المقتضي من الموانع فلو فرض حينئذ عدم رفع المانع لزم خلاف المفروض ومنه تمنع الحركة مع أنها واقعة اه منه","english":"On “Together with the unimpeded operation of what necessitates it.” He says “unimpeded operation,” not merely “existence,” to indicate that motion does not occur unless its necessitating cause is free of impediments. To suppose at that point that the impediment was not removed would contradict the supposition; motion would thereby be prevented although it is occurring. End. [Signed:] “From him.”","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I269.jpg","stage":69,"editorial":"The impersonal signature منه does not identify the annotator.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I269.upper.01","volume":1,"page":269,"physical_pdf_page":282,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"إذا نسبت إلى حركة أخرى (تسمى باعتبار الشدة) وقلة الزمان بالنسبة إليها (سرعة وباعتبار الضعف) وكثرة الزمان بالنسبة إليها (بطأ) ثم المعاوقة التي تكون في نفس المتحرك كثقل الجسم تصير سببا لبطء الحركة القسرية كما في تحريك أحد الصخرة العظيمة إلى فوق والإرادية كما في صعود الإنسان الجبل لا الطبيعية لامتناع أن يكون الشيء مقتضيا لأمر ومانعا منه والمعاوقة التي تكون في الخارج كغلظ قوام ما يتحرك فيه تصير سببا لبطء الحركة الطبيعية كنزول الحجر في الماء والقسرية والإرادية كحركة السهم والإنسان فيه وقد يكون السبب في بطئها نفس الإرادة كما في رحى الحجر وتحريك اليد برفق","english":"—when compared with another motion, it “is called speed in respect of intensity” and the smaller amount of time relative to the other motion, “and slowness in respect of weakness” and the greater amount of time. Resistance within the moving subject, such as bodily heaviness, causes forced motion to be slow—as when someone raises a large rock—and voluntary motion also, as when a person climbs a mountain; but not natural motion, since a thing cannot both necessitate something and impede it. External resistance, such as the thick consistency of the medium, causes slowness in natural motion, as when a stone descends through water, and in forced and voluntary motion, as with an arrow or a person moving through it. Volition itself may cause slowness, as in turning a millstone or moving one’s hand gently.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I269.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I268.upper.02","readings":[{"locus":"كما في رحى الحجر","status":"The millstone example is legible but its abbreviated construction requires checking; no additional mechanism is supplied."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I269.upper.02","volume":1,"page":269,"physical_pdf_page":282,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"ولا خلاف في سببية هذه الأمور في الجملة لكن عند المتكلمين من جهة أنه يكثر حينئذ تخلل السكنات التي لا تخلو الحركة من نوعها وتختلف بالسرعة والبطء بسبب قلتها وكثرتها وعند الفلاسفة من جهة أنها تصير سببا لضعف الميل الذي هو العلة القريبة للحركة فضعف المعلول (وليس) هو بتخلل السكنات بين الحركات لوجوه ثلاثة الأول أن في الحركة البطيئة علة الحركة موجودة بشرائطها والموانع مرتفعة (لامتناع عدم الحركة مع خلوص المقتضي لها وعدم رفع المانع) أي لو لم تكن الموانع مرتفعة لامتنعت الحركة وإن كان المقتضي وهو الميل موجودا لكنها واقعة في البطيئة فلا بد مع وجود المقتضي لها من ارتفاع موانعها أيضا فلو","english":"There is no dispute that these factors are causes in a general sense. According to the theologians, however, they operate by increasing the interspersed rests: motion never lacks rests of this kind, and differs in speed and slowness through their fewer or greater number. According to the philosophers, these factors weaken the inclination that is motion’s proximate cause, and consequently weaken its effect. “It is not” through rests interspersed between motions. They give three reasons. First, in slow motion the cause of motion exists with its conditions, while impediments are absent, “because the absence of motion is impossible together with the unimpeded operation of what necessitates it and the failure to remove the impediment.” That is: if impediments had not been removed, motion would be impossible even though its necessitating cause—inclination—existed. But slow motion does occur; therefore, together with its necessitating cause, its impediments must also be absent. Thus, if—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I269.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I270.upper.01","readings":[{"locus":"مع خلوص المقتضي لها وعدم رفع المانع","status":"The apparent conjunction yields a difficult formulation. Both printed note registers discuss the wording; retained rather than silently rewritten. The explanatory sentence contrasts an operative cause with the removal of impediments."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I270.lower.01","volume":1,"page":270,"physical_pdf_page":283,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله لزم تخلف المعلول الخ) ورد عليه أنه يجوز وجود المانع في وقت وارتفاعه في آخر من غير شعور لنا بذلك ويمكن أن يقال إنا نعلم أن حالنا في حركتنا الإرادية مختلف بالنسبة إلى الميل المقتضي لها اه منه","english":"On “An effect would fail to follow, etc.” It is objected that an impediment may exist at one time and disappear at another without our being aware of it. One may reply that we know our condition in voluntary motion differs in respect of the inclination necessitating that motion. End. [Signed:] “From him.”","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I270.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I270.lower.02","volume":1,"page":270,"physical_pdf_page":283,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله والثاني أن البطء الخ) وفيه أن المقصد حركة واحدة لشيء واحد مركب من أجزاء مادية بحيث صارت جسما واحدا فالسرعة والبطء ههنا إنما يكون بالنسبة إلى حركة أخرى لجسم آخر لا لبعض أجزائه بالنسبة إلى البعض الآخر اه منه","english":"On “Second, that slowness, etc.” The difficulty is that the subject under discussion is one motion of one thing composed of material parts that have become one body. Speed and slowness here are relative to another motion of another body, not relative to some of its own parts as compared with other parts. End. [Signed:] “From him.”","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I270.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I270.lower.03","volume":1,"page":270,"physical_pdf_page":283,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله فقد لا يتحرك الثاني الخ) وإنما يلزم ذلك لو انحصر سبب البطء عندهم في ذلك وأنه ممنوع اه منه","english":"On “The second might not move, etc.” That follows only if, according to them, the cause of slowness is confined to this; that restriction is denied. End. [Signed:] “From him.”","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I270.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I270.lower.04","volume":1,"page":270,"physical_pdf_page":283,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله لزم الانفكاك الخ) أقول هذا مشترك الورود فإنه لو كان سبب اختلاف الحركتين اختلاف الميل لزم الانفكاك فبمثل ذلك أيضا لامتناع أن يكون بعض أجزاء الجسم الواحد أسرع وبعضها أبطأ بدون الانفكاك فاختلاف الميل في جسم واحد غير معقول اه منه","english":"On “Separation would follow, etc.” I say: the objection applies equally [to the other account]. If the difference between the two motions resulted from a difference of inclination, separation would likewise follow. It is impossible for some parts of a single body to be faster and others slower without separation; differing inclination within a single body is thus unintelligible. End. [Signed:] “From him.”","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I270.jpg","stage":69,"readings":[{"locus":"فبمثل ذلك أيضا","status":"This brief connector is not secure; the reciprocal objection itself is fully represented."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I270.lower_continuation.01","volume":1,"page":270,"physical_pdf_page":283,"layer":"lower_continuation","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"مضطربة والظاهر أن لفظة الخلوص زائدة من النسخ أو تقديم لفظة العدم عليها فتأمل","english":"—confused. Apparently, the word “unimpededness” (khulūṣ) was added during copying, or the word “absence” should precede it. Consider this.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I270.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I269.lower.02","editorial":"The lowest ruled compartment continues the preceding leaf’s lower note. These are the printed annotator’s proposed repairs, not emendations silently adopted by this edition.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I270.upper.01","volume":1,"page":270,"physical_pdf_page":283,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"وقع مع ذلك سكون هناك لزم تخلف المعلول عن تمام العلة والثاني أن البطء لو كان بتخلل السكنات لامتنع تلازم الحركتين مع اتحاد الزمان واختلاف المسافة لأن الحركة التي في المسافة القصيرة تكون أبطأ فتكون تخلل السكنات فيها أكثر فقد لا يتحرك الثاني عند تحرك الأول (و) ذلك محال لاستلزامه (لزوم الانفكاك في مثل حركتي طرفي الرحى) أي الدائرة العظيمة والصغيرة (و) الثالث أنه لو كان سبب البطء تخلل السكنات لزم (زيادة سكنات الطائر) مثلا (على حركاته بما لا يحصى) لأن الفلك الأعظم تقطع في يوم وليلة الأبعد وجه الأرض وجميع وجه الأرض بالنسبة إلى الفلك الأعظم ليس بقدر محسوس فلزم أن يتخلل حركة الطائر سكنات بقدر زمان حركة الفلك الأعظم عليها فتكون حركته مغمورة لا يحس بها فنرى الطائر ساكنا أو يحس بها في زمان أقل من زمان السكنات بتلك النسبة لأن السكون وإن كان عدميا لكنه لا خفاء في أن الجسم قد يرى متحركا وقد يرى ساكنا ويفرق الحس","english":"—rest occurred despite that, an effect would fail to follow upon its complete cause. Second, if slowness resulted from interspersed rests, two motions could not necessarily accompany each other over the same time while traversing different distances. The motion over the shorter distance is slower; it would therefore contain more rests, and the second [moving point] might not move while the first did. “And” this is impossible, since it entails “separation in such a case as the motions of the two extremities of a millstone”—the larger and smaller circles. “And,” third, if interspersed rests caused slowness, “a bird’s rests,” for example, would “exceed its movements beyond reckoning.” The greatest sphere traverses in a day and a night [the expanse described in the difficult printed clause]; the whole face of the earth is of no sensible magnitude in comparison with it. A bird’s motion would therefore have rests interspersed for a time corresponding to the greatest sphere’s motion over those [distances]. Its movement would be overwhelmed and imperceptible, so we would see the bird at rest; or its motion would be perceived during a time smaller than its rests in that proportion. Although rest is privative, it is evident that a body is sometimes seen moving and sometimes stationary, and sense distinguishes—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I270.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I269.upper.02","join_out":"I271.upper.01","readings":[{"locus":"تقطع في يوم وليلة الأبعد وجه الأرض","status":"The phrase between يوم وليلة and وجه الأرض is not securely read. The huge ratio of celestial to terrestrial motion is clear; an exact distance has not been invented."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I271.lower.01","volume":1,"page":271,"physical_pdf_page":284,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله كل حركتين الخ) أي سواء كانتا مستقيمتين أو مستديرتين وسواء كانت الثانية رجوعا إلى الصوب الأول كما في الصاعدة والهابطة أو انعطافا إلى صوب آخر","english":"On “Every two motions, etc.” That is, whether the two are rectilinear or circular, and whether the second is a return towards the original direction, as in ascent followed by descent, or a turn towards another direction.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I271.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I271.lower.02","volume":1,"page":271,"physical_pdf_page":284,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله لأن الوصول إلى المنتهى آني الخ) إذ لو كان زمانيا ففي نصف ذلك الزمان إما أن يحصل الوصول فلا يكون الوصول في ذلك الزمان بل في نصفه هذا خلف أو لا يحصل فلا يكون المفروض زمان الوصول هذا خلف","english":"On “Because arrival at the endpoint is instantaneous, etc.” If it occupied a time, arrival would either occur in half of that time, in which case its time would be that half rather than the time supposed—a contradiction—or it would not occur, in which case the supposed interval would not be a time of arrival—again a contradiction.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I271.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I271.lower.03","volume":1,"page":271,"physical_pdf_page":284,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله والرجوع عن المنتهى الخ) أي ابتداء الرجوع الذي يعبر عنه باللاوصول والمباينة فلا يرد أن الرجوع حركة وهي زمانية لا آنية","english":"On “Departure back from the endpoint, etc.” He means the beginning of return, expressed as “non-arrival” or separation. It is therefore no objection that returning is a motion and is temporal rather than instantaneous.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I271.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I271.lower.04","volume":1,"page":271,"physical_pdf_page":284,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله ضرورة امتناع الميل الخ) أقول قد قرر بعضهم هذه الحجة على وجه بين الشارح «مد ظله» بيان خلاصته في تحريره لها ملخصه لكن ادعى ذلك البعض الضرورة في تغاير الآنين ولما كان منع ضرورة تغايرهما في غاية الظهور لجواز أن يقع الوصول واللاوصول أعني نهاية حركة الذهاب وبداية حركة الرجعي في آن","english":"On “Since inclination is necessarily impossible, etc.” I say: someone has formulated this proof in a manner whose gist the commentator—may his protection endure—sets out in his presentation. But that person asserted that the two instants necessarily differ. Denying the alleged necessity is very straightforward, since arrival and non-arrival—that is, the end of the outgoing motion and the beginning of the returning motion—may occur at an instant—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I271.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I272.lower.01","readings":[{"locus":"على وجه بين الشارح ... في تحريره لها ملخصه","status":"The short description of the commentator’s restatement is syntactically compressed; the ensuing objection is clear and translated continuously."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I271.ruled.01","volume":1,"page":271,"physical_pdf_page":284,"layer":"ruled","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله وأجيب عن الأول الخ) حاصل الجواب منع كون الميل علة موجبة بل العلة الموجبة هو الله تعالى اه منه","english":"On “It is answered against the first, etc.” The gist is to deny that inclination is a necessitating cause. Rather, the necessitating cause is God, exalted is He. End. [Signed:] “From him.”","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I271.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I271.upper.01","volume":1,"page":271,"physical_pdf_page":284,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"بين الحالتين (وأجيب) عن الأول (بأن الحركة) بل جميع الممكنات (بمحض خلق الله) فله أن يوجد الحركة في زمان والسكون في آخر مع كون المحرك بحاله (و) عن الثاني (أن الانفكاك ثم الالتئام جائز) ولا نسلم تلازم الحركتين بمعنى امتناع الانفكاك عقلا وإنما هو عادي يجوز ارتفاعه (و) عن الثالث (أن الحركات لكونها وجودية) ومع ذلك كانت (متجددة) شيئا فشيئا (متميزة) في الحس (عن السكنات) بل غالبة عليها بحيث تسترها (وإن كانت) السكنات (أضعاف آلافها) فنرى الطير متحركا دائما وفي شرح المقاصد لا يخفى قوة الأدلة وضعف الأجوبة","english":"—the two conditions. “It is answered,” against the first argument, “that motion”—indeed, all contingent things—“is solely through God’s creation.” He may therefore create motion at one time and rest at another while the mover remains as it was. “And,” against the second, “separation followed by rejoining is possible.” We do not concede that the two motions necessarily accompany one another in the sense that separation is rationally impossible: their conjunction is only habitual, and may cease. “And,” against the third, “because motions are positive existents” and, moreover, “are renewed” successively, they are sensibly “distinguished from rests”; indeed, they predominate over them and conceal them, “even though” the rests “are many thousands of times” more numerous. Hence we always see the bird moving. In Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid: “The strength of the arguments and the weakness of the replies are evident.”","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I271.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I270.upper.01","editorial":"The final evaluation is reported as a quotation from Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid; the preceding replies are not endorsed by the translation.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I271.upper.02","volume":1,"page":271,"physical_pdf_page":284,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(قالوا) أي الفلاسفة (لا بد بين كل حركتين) كصاعدة وهابطة (من سكون لأن) الوصول إلى المنتهى آني فإن الحد الذي هو منتهى المسافة الممتدة لا يكون منقسما في ذلك الامتداد وإلا لم يكن بتمامه حدا والرجوع عن المنتهى أيضا آني و(آن الوصول غير آن الرجوع) ضرورة امتناع اجتماع الميل إلى الشيء","english":"“They say”—the philosophers—“that between every two motions,” such as ascent and descent, “there must be rest, because” arrival at the endpoint is instantaneous. The limit terminating an extended distance is not divisible in that extension; otherwise, the whole of it would not be a limit. Departure back from the endpoint is likewise instantaneous. “The instant of arrival differs from the instant of return,” since it is necessarily impossible for an inclination towards a thing—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I271.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I272.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I272.lower.01","volume":1,"page":272,"physical_pdf_page":285,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"واحد وهو حد مشترك بين زمانيهما كالنقطة الواحدة التي تكون بداية خط ونهاية آخر ولا يقتضي ذلك أن يصدق على الشيء أنه واصل وليس بواصل حتى يمتنع بل إنما يقتضي أن يحصل له الوصول وابتداء الرجوع الذي هو لا وصول وذلك جائز كما يحصل لجسم الحركة والسواد الذي هو لا حركة قررها من آخر بوجه آخر وهو أن الحركة إنما تصدر من علة موجودة تسمى ميلا وذلك الميل هو العلة للوصول إلى الحد فيكون موجودا في آن الوصول إذ ليس من الأمور التي لا توجد إلا في الزمان كالحركة ثم المباينة عن ذلك الحد لا توجد إلا بعد حدوث ميل ثان في آن ثان ضرورة امتناع الميل إلى الشيء والميل منه في آن واحد والجواب الذي ذكره المصنف وحرره الشارح كما يأتي إنما هو جواب ذكره من الجهة على التقدير الثاني فتدبره إذا ظهر هذا ظهر فرق تغاير بين سوابق كلام الشارح «مد ظله» ولواحقه وإن أمكن الجمع بينهما بنوع تكلف تفسير","english":"—one instant, the common boundary between their two times, like a single point that is the beginning of one line and the end of another. This does not require that a thing be truly described as both arriving and not arriving, which would be impossible. It requires only that arrival and the beginning of return—which is non-arrival—occur to it. That is permissible, just as motion and blackness, which is not motion, may occur to a body. Another formulation proceeds differently: motion issues only from an existent cause called inclination. That inclination causes arrival at the limit and therefore exists at the instant of arrival, since it is not, like motion, among things existing only in time. Separation from that limit then occurs only after a second inclination arises at a second instant, because an inclination towards a thing and an inclination away from it cannot coexist in one instant. The author’s answer, expounded by the commentator below, addresses the proof on this second formulation. Consider it. Once this is apparent, a difference emerges between the earlier and later portions of the commentator’s explanation—may his protection endure—although they may be reconciled through a somewhat strained interpretation.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I272.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I271.lower.04","readings":[{"locus":"قررها من آخر / جواب ذكره من الجهة / فرق تغاير","status":"Several short reporting expressions are indistinct; the two formulations of the argument and their different targets remain separately translated."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I272.lower.02","volume":1,"page":272,"physical_pdf_page":285,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله وتركب الزمان من الآنات الخ) إشارة إلى دفع ما يقال أن تحقق الآن لم يستلزم وجود الجزء وتتالي الآنات يستلزمه وحاصل الدفع أنه على","english":"On “Time would be composed of instants, etc.” This indicates an answer to the objection: the reality of an instant does not entail an indivisible part, whereas the immediate succession of instants does. The gist of the answer is that, on—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I272.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I273.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I272.ruled.01","volume":1,"page":272,"physical_pdf_page":285,"layer":"ruled","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله ولو سلم فلا نسلم الخ) بناء على أن هذا يستلزم جواز انقسامه بالفعل والمفروض خلافه اه منه","english":"On “Even if granted, we do not concede, etc.” This is based on the fact that this entails the possibility of its actual division, whereas the hypothesis is otherwise. End. [Signed:] “From him.”","reading_status":"second_visual_read","source_image":"source_images/I272.jpg","stage":69,"proof_notes":["Enlarged strip reads أن هذا يستلزم, not أنهما; demonstrative and number agreement resolved."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I272.upper.01","volume":1,"page":272,"physical_pdf_page":285,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"مع الميل عنه (فلولا زمان السكون بينهما) أي زمان يكون الجسم فيه عديم الميل فيكون عديم الحركة (لزم تتالي الآنين) وتركب الزمان من الآنات بزيادة واحد واحد (المستلزم لوجود الجزء) لكون الزمان منطبقا على الحركة المنطبقة على المسافة (وأجيب) بعد تسلم نفي الجزء وكون الميل علة موجبة لا معدة حتى لو كان آن الوصول هو آن الرجوع لزم اجتماع الميل إلى الشيء مع الميل عنه وذلك خلاف الضرورة (بأنه لا آن) عندكم (بدون الانقطاع) أي انقطاع الزمان لأنه عندكم عبارة عن طرف الزمان ونهايته فكيف يقع فيه الوصول والرجوع وإن أريد به زمان لا ينقسم إلا وهما فلا نسلم تغاير آني الميلين لجواز أن يتعاقبا في آن واحد بحسب ما له من الانقسام الوهمي ولو سلم فلا نسلم استحالة تتالي الآنات بهذا المعنى وإنما يستحيل لو لزم منه الجزء الذي لا ينقسم بالوهم أيضا وليس كذلك كما لا يخفى","english":"—to coexist with an inclination away from it. “Were there no time of rest between them”—a time during which the body has no inclination and therefore no motion—“two instants would immediately succeed one another.” Time would be composed of instants added one by one, “entailing the existence of an indivisible part,” since time corresponds to motion, which corresponds to distance. “It is answered”—after conceding the denial of the indivisible part and that inclination is a necessitating, not merely preparatory, cause, so that identifying the instant of arrival with the instant of return would entail inclinations towards and away from the same thing together, contrary to necessity—“that there is no instant,” on your account, “without interruption”: interruption of time, since an instant, on your account, means a temporal extremity and end. How, then, could arrival and return occur in it? If “instant” means a time divisible only in imagination, we do not concede that the instants of the two inclinations differ: the inclinations may succeed one another within one such instant according to its imagined divisions. Even if granted, we do not concede that instants in this sense cannot immediately succeed one another. That impossibility would hold only if an indivisible part, incapable even of imagined division, followed; and that is not the case, as is evident.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","source_image":"source_images/I272.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I271.upper.02","proof_notes":["Enlarged upper source resolves بزيادة واحد واحد, بأنه لا آن, تغاير آني الميلين, في آن واحد, and لو لزم منه. These resolve the substantive ambiguity: the denial concerns distinct instants of the inclinations, not distinct inclinations themselves."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I273.lower.01","volume":1,"page":273,"physical_pdf_page":286,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"تقدير التتالي يكون الامتداد الذي هو مقدار الحركة المنطبقة على المسافة متألفا من الآنات بزيادة واحد واحد ولا كذلك تحقق الطرف للزمان الذي هو عرض غير حال فيه حلول السريان وهذا مثل ما يقال ثبوت النقطة لا يستلزم الجزء وتألف الخط من النقط يستلزمه وقد يقال لو تقرر هذا لا يخلو إما أن يلزم تتالي الآنات أو تخلل السكنات في كل حركة مما إذا كان التحرك لا يماشي المسافة إلا بنقطة نقطة على التوالي كما أنا أدركنا كرة على سطح مستو فإن آن الوصول إلى كل نقطة مغاير آن المباينة عنها وأجيب بأن انقسام المسافة محض توهم فلا تحقق للنقطة والآن بخلاف ما إذا انقطعت الحركة فتحققت لها نهاية فإنه لا بد منها في المسافة أيضا لانطباقها عليها قال في شرح المقاصد وفيه نظر لا يخفى","english":"—the supposition of immediate succession, the extension measuring motion, which corresponds to distance, would be composed of instants added one by one. This differs from a boundary obtaining for time, which is an accident not inhering in it by pervasive inherence. It is like saying that the existence of a point does not entail an indivisible part, whereas composing a line from points does. One might object: if this is established, every motion must involve either immediately successive instants or interspersed rests. The moving thing proceeds over the distance point by point in succession—for example, when we roll a sphere across a level surface. The instant of arrival at each point differs from the instant of departure from it. The answer is that the division of the distance is merely imagined: no point or instant is actually established. This differs from motion being interrupted, when it obtains an endpoint; because motion corresponds to distance, that endpoint must also obtain in the distance. In Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid: “There is an evident difficulty in this.”","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I273.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I272.lower.02","readings":[{"locus":"مما إذا كان التحرك لا يماشي / أدركنا كرة","status":"The words introducing traversal and the sphere example are not securely transcribed. The example visibly concerns a sphere on a level surface; exact verbs remain to be checked."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I273.lower.02","volume":1,"page":273,"physical_pdf_page":286,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله والتقدير عدم القاسر) أي إلى السكون (قوله القوتين الصاعدة والهابطة الخ) الأولى الدافعة والراجعة فتدبر","english":"On “The hypothesis excludes any constraining agent”: that is, any agent constraining to rest. On “The ascending and descending forces, etc.” “The propelling and returning forces” would be preferable. Reflect.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I273.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I273.lower.03","volume":1,"page":273,"physical_pdf_page":286,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله وإنما ترجع الخ)","english":"On “It only turns back, etc.”—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I273.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I274.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I273.paratext.01","volume":1,"page":273,"physical_pdf_page":286,"layer":"paratext","attribution":"printer’s paratext","arabic":"(١٨ ـ تقريب أول)","english":"Printer’s gathering mark: “18 — Taqrīb, first [volume].”","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I273.jpg","stage":69,"editorial":"Recorded separately; not an argumentative sentence or an authored marginal gloss.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I273.upper.01","volume":1,"page":273,"physical_pdf_page":286,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(وعورض بأنه لو لزم) السكون بين الحركتين (لكان) لها سكونا طبيعيا وهو ظاهر البطلان أو قسريا والتقدير عدم القاسر سواء كان إرادة أو غيرها فكان السكون (بلا سبب) وإنه محال (و) لكان هو (لا في زمان معين) لأن كل زمان نفرض فأقل منه كاف في دفع تتالي الآنين لقبوله الانقسام إلى غير النهاية فامتنع كونه في زمان تام وإلا لزم الترجيح بلا مرجح (و) أيضا لو لزم ذلك (لوقف الميل الهابط بملاقاة خردلة صاعدة) ليتوسط السكون بين حركتي الصعود والهبوط للخردلة ووقوف الجبل بملاقاة الخردلة لا يقبله العقل","english":"“It is countered that, if” rest between two motions “were necessary,” it “would be” a natural rest for the body—which is plainly false—or a forced rest, although the hypothesis excludes any constraining agent, whether volition or something else. Rest would therefore occur “without a cause,” which is impossible. “And” it would occur “in no determinate time,” since, however small a time we suppose, a smaller one suffices to prevent two instants from immediately succeeding: it admits division without end. Rest could not therefore occupy a complete [determinate] time, or an ungrounded preference would result. “And,” furthermore, if this were necessary, “a descending [mountain] would stop on encountering an ascending mustard seed,” so that rest could intervene between the seed’s ascending and descending motions. Reason does not accept that a mountain stops upon meeting a mustard seed.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I273.jpg","stage":69,"readings":[{"locus":"لوقف الميل الهابط","status":"The lemma’s noun resembles الميل in the scan, but the surrounding explanation and the following page unmistakably discuss الجبل, a mountain. Translation identifies the referent in brackets, without claiming a collated variant."},{"locus":"زمان تام","status":"The phrase is read provisionally; the point is the absence of a uniquely determined interval."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I273.upper.02","volume":1,"page":273,"physical_pdf_page":286,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(وأجيب) عن الأول (بأن السبب) للسكون (عدم الحركة) عند تعادل القوتين الصاعدة والهابطة القاسر إلى السكون (و) عن الثاني (بأنه يقع) السكون (في زمان لا ينقسم فعلا) وإن انقسم وهما والزمان الغير المنقسم بالفعل يمتنع أن يكون بعضه مقدار السكون وبعضه لا وإلا لزم الانقسام بالفعل وهو خلاف المفروض (و) عن الثالث (بأن الخردلة ترجع) بمصادمة هواء الجبل فسكون الخردلة إنما هو قبيل ملاقاة الجبل ولو سلم فوقوف الجبل مستبعد لا مستحيل","english":"“It is answered,” against the first, “that the cause” of rest “is the absence of motion” when the ascending and descending forces balance, constraining [the body] to rest. “And,” against the second, “that” rest “occurs in a time not actually divided,” although divisible in imagination. In a time not actually divided, one part cannot measure the rest while another does not; that would introduce an actual division, contrary to the hypothesis. “And,” against the third, “that the mustard seed turns back” on colliding with the mountain’s air. Its rest therefore occurs just before meeting the mountain. Even if [the encounter] is conceded, the mountain’s stopping is improbable, not impossible.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I273.jpg","stage":69,"readings":[{"locus":"عدم الحركة / القاسر","status":"The printed wording is retained. The margin qualifies the pairing of the upward and downward forces and should be read with this answer."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I274.lower.01","volume":1,"page":274,"physical_pdf_page":287,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"أي بناء على أنه قد يشاهد أن الملاقاة تكون حال الصعود دون الرجوع فعلم أن الرجوع لا يكون إلا بعد الملاقاة","english":"That is, on the basis that the encounter is sometimes observed during ascent, not during return. It is therefore known that return occurs only after the encounter.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I274.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I273.lower.03","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I274.lower.02","volume":1,"page":274,"physical_pdf_page":287,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله لإحدى الحركتين على الأخرى الخ) فإن كان ذلك الفضل لحركة السفينة مثلا يرى بطيئة وإن كان لحركة الشخص يرى راجعا","english":"On “The excess of one motion over the other, etc.” If the excess belongs, for example, to the ship’s motion, [the person] appears to move slowly; if it belongs to the person’s motion, he appears to move back.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I274.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I274.lower.03","volume":1,"page":274,"physical_pdf_page":287,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله بقاء النسب الحاصلة الخ) إن قيل يصدق هذا على السكون في الوضع فإنه كما يأتي عبارة عن بقاء الوضع أي النسب الحاصلة للجسم إلى أشياء ذوات وضع قلت السكون عبارة عن بقاء تلك النسب من حيث إنها في المكان كالدليل عليه قوله بأن يكون الجسم مستقرا في مكان الخ بخلاف الوضع فإنه عبارة عن بقائها لا من تلك الحيثية فافترقا","english":"On “Persistence of the relations obtaining, etc.” If it is said that this also applies to rest in position, which, as follows, is the persistence of position—that is, the body’s relations to things having position—I reply: [local] rest is the persistence of those relations insofar as they concern place. His words, “The body remaining settled in a place,” indicate this. Positional [rest], by contrast, is their persistence without that particular consideration. The two therefore differ.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I274.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I274.lower.04","volume":1,"page":274,"physical_pdf_page":287,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله عما من شأنه الخ) يخرج عدم حركة الأعراض والمفارقات والأجسام في آن ابتداء","english":"On “From what is capable, etc.” This excludes the absence of motion in accidents, separate entities, and bodies at the instant of initial—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I274.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I275.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I274.upper.01","volume":1,"page":274,"physical_pdf_page":287,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(فصل) قد يتحرك الجسم حركتين إلى جهة واحدة وقد يتحرك إلى جهتين أو جهات مختلفة (فإذا تحرك الجسم) بحركتين إلى جهة كالمتحرك في سفينة إلى الجهة التي تتحرك إليها السفينة فبعده عن المبدأ بقدر الحركتين وإذا تحرك (إلى جهتين متقابلتين) كالمتحرك في السفينة إلى خلاف جهتها (فبعده عن المبدأ بقدر الفضل) لإحدى الحركتين على الأخرى (وإلا) لم يكن لإحداهما فضل (فـ) يرى أنه (يسكن) في المبدأ ولا يتحرك وإذا تحرك إلى جهتين غير متقابلتين كالمتحرك شمالا في سفينة تجري غربا فبعده عنه مائلا إلى الجهتين بقدر الحركتين وإذا تحرك إلى جهات كحركة شخص في سفينة تدفع شمالا في ماء يجري غربا وتحرك الريح جنوبا فيكون متوسطا بين الجهات على حسب اقتضاء المحركات","english":"Section. A body may undergo two motions towards one direction, or motions towards two or more different directions. “If a body moves” through two motions in one direction, as when someone walks aboard a ship in the direction in which the ship moves, its distance from the starting point equals the amount of both motions. If it moves “towards two opposite directions,” as when someone walks aboard the ship against its direction, “its distance from the starting point equals the excess” of one motion over the other. “Otherwise,” if neither exceeds the other, “it” appears to “remain at rest” at the starting point and not move. If it moves towards two directions that are not opposite, as when someone walks north aboard a ship travelling west, its distance from the starting point is oblique towards both directions, according to the two motions. With several directions—as when someone moves aboard a ship pushed north in water flowing west, while wind moves [it] south—the resultant is intermediate between the directions according to what the movers require.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I274.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I274.upper.02","volume":1,"page":274,"physical_pdf_page":287,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(والسكون) كالحركة يقع في المقولات الأربع (في الأين بقاء النسب) الحاصلة للجسم إلى أشياء ذوات أوضاع بأن يكون الجسم مستقرا في مكان واحد (وفي غيره) أي الثلاث الباقية (بقاء النوع) الحاصل بالفعل من غير تغير وذلك بأن يبقى في الكم من غير نمو ولا ذبول ولا تخلخل وتكاثف وفي الكيف من غير اشتداد وضعف وفي الوضع من غير تبدل إلى وضع آخر (فهو) بهذا المعنى وجودي (يضاد الحركة وقيل) هو (عدم الحركة) عما من شأنه أن يكون متحركا (فـ) يكون (عدم ملكة) للحركة (و) هو","english":"“Rest,” like motion, occurs in the four categories. “In place it is the persistence of the relations” obtaining between the body and things having positions, the body remaining settled in one place. “In the others”—the remaining three—“it is the persistence of the species” actually obtained without alteration: in quantity, without growth, wasting, rarefaction or condensation; in quality, without intensification or weakening; and in position, without a change to another position. “Thus,” in this sense, it is positive and “contrary to motion. It has also been said” that it is “the absence of motion” from what is capable of moving. “Thus” it is “privation” of motion, “and” it is—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I274.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I275.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I275.corrector.01","volume":1,"page":275,"physical_pdf_page":288,"layer":"corrector","attribution":"unnamed printed corrector","arabic":"(٢) لعل في العبارة نقصا فتأمل كتبه مصححه","english":"“(2) Perhaps something is missing from the expression; consider it. Written by its corrector.”","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I275.jpg","stage":69,"editorial":"The signature names a function, not a person; this is positive evidence of a source-level problem, not evidence of a specific manuscript variant.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I275.lower.01","volume":1,"page":275,"physical_pdf_page":288,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"الحركة وإنما لم يقدر (قوله كالحركة الخ) إلا أن الحركة الطبيعية تفتقر إلى أمر آخر غير الطبيعة أعني مفارقة أمر غير طبيعي بخلاف السكون الطبيعي فإنه مستند إلى الطبيعة ولا يفتقر إلى تلك المفارقة فإن الجسم إذا خلي وطبعه لم يكن له بد من موضع معين لا ذاهب مفارقته","english":"—motion, [the brief closing expression is unclear]. On “Like motion, etc.” Natural motion, however, requires something besides nature, namely separation from an unnatural condition. Natural rest, by contrast, depends upon nature and does not require that separation: when a body is left to its nature, it must have a determinate position, [the closing phrase concerning departure from it is insecure].","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I275.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I274.lower.04","readings":[{"locus":"وإنما لم يقدر / معين لا ذاهب مفارقته","status":"The short opening and closing phrases need a clearer reading; the distinction between natural motion and rest is retained without inventing the damaged connectors."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I275.lower.02","volume":1,"page":275,"physical_pdf_page":288,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله ويتضاد بتضاد الخ) المراد بالتضاد مطلق امتناع الاجتماع ليصح على قول من يجعله عدم ملكة أيضا ثم إنهم قالوا الاختلاف في تقابل الحركة والسكون في المكان (٢) فهو الحركة من المبدأ أو المنتهى أو كليهما والذي هو الأخير فيهما فإن قيل إن السكون في المنتهى لكل الحركة وكل الذي لا يكون مقابلا له أجيب بمنع الصغرى فإن السكون كالحركة لا حركة فافهم","english":"On “Its contrariety follows the contrariety, etc.” “Contrariety” here means incompatibility in general, so that the statement is also correct on the view making rest a privation. They then discuss the opposition between motion and rest in place [the printed sentence here is incomplete or disordered]. Is it motion from the starting point, the endpoint, or both? [The ensuing objection and reply about rest at the endpoint are textually insecure.] Understand this.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I275.jpg","stage":69,"readings":[{"locus":"الاختلاف في تقابل ... لا حركة","status":"A substantial portion of this short argument cannot be securely parsed from the scan. Its entire provisional transcription is retained; the original corrector explicitly notes a deficiency. This unit is NOT a gap-free translation."}],"editorial":"See I275.corrector.01, the printed correction notice attached to this passage.","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I275.lower.03","volume":1,"page":275,"physical_pdf_page":288,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله في المكان الأعلى الخ) قد عرفت أن التضاد بين المكان الأعلى والأسفل ليس بالذات بل باعتبار عارضي القرب والبعد كما سبق","english":"On “In a higher place, etc.” You already know that the contrariety between a higher and a lower place is not intrinsic, but is through the incidental attributes of nearness and distance, as explained earlier.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I275.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I275.lower.04","volume":1,"page":275,"physical_pdf_page":288,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله معقولة بالقياس إلى الأولى الخ) أي لا يتم تعقلها إلا بتعقلها حتى أن تعقلهما يكون معا بحيث لا تقدم أحدهما على الآخر فيخرج ما يكون تعقله مستلزما ومستتبعا لتعقل شيء آخر كالملزومات بالنسبة إلى لوازمها البينة","english":"On “Conceived in comparison with the first, etc.” That is, conceiving one is not completed except by conceiving the other, so that the two are conceived together and neither precedes the other. This excludes cases where conceiving one thing entails and brings with it the conception of something else, as things entailing self-evident concomitants do in relation to those concomitants.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I275.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I275.lower.05","volume":1,"page":275,"physical_pdf_page":288,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله كالأب مع وصف الأبوة الخ) أي كالذات المعتبر معها تلك الصفة وأما إطلاق المضاف على الذات وحدها كما نقل عن المواقف فغير مشهور وإن صح على قانون اللغة كذا قالوا (قوله وقد تتخالفان الخ) والاختلاف قد يكون محدودا كما في الضعفية والنصفية","english":"On “Such as a father qualified by fatherhood, etc.” That is, a subject considered together with that attribute. Applying “relative” to the subject alone, as reported from al-Mawāqif, is not customary, although linguistically permissible. Such is their account. On “Or may differ, etc.” The difference may be determinate, as in being double and being half—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I275.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I276.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I275.upper.01","volume":1,"page":275,"physical_pdf_page":288,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"كالحركة أيضا في أنه (يكون طبيعيا) كسكون الحجر على الأرض (وقسريا) كسكونه معلقا في الهواء (وإراديا) كوقوف الطير في الهواء (ويتضاد بتضاد ما فيه) ولا عبرة بتضاد الساكن والمسكن والزمان كالأربعة في الحركة ولا تعلق للسكون بما منه وما إليه فلذلك (كالسكون في المكان الأعلى والأسفل)","english":"—also like motion in that “it may be natural,” as a stone resting on the earth; “forced,” as when the stone is suspended in the air; “or voluntary,” as when a bird remains stationary in the air. “Its contrariety follows the contrariety of that in which it occurs.” The contrariety of the resting subject, the agent causing rest, and the time is irrelevant, as with the four [other factors] in motion. Rest has no relation to a starting point and endpoint. Hence [the example]: “rest in a higher and a lower place.”","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I275.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I274.upper.02","readings":[{"locus":"كالأربعة في الحركة","status":"The phrase refers back to the requisites excluded from determining contrariety; its precise enumeration should be checked against the earlier motion discussion."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I275.upper.02","volume":1,"page":275,"physical_pdf_page":288,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(فصل) في (الإضافة) و(هي النسبة المنعكسة) التي لا تتعقل إلا بالقياس إلى نسبة أخرى معقولة بالقياس إلى الأولى كالأبوة والبنوة (وهذه النسبة) تسمى (مضافا حقيقيا) كما تسمى إضافة (والمجموع المركب منه) أي من الحقيقي (ومن المعروض له) كالأب مع وصف الأبوة والابن مع وصف البنوة يسمى مضافا (مشهوريا والنسبتان) المتعاكستان (قد تتوافقان) من الجانبين كالأخوة (وقد تتخالفان)","english":"Section on “relation.” “It is the reciprocal relation” which can be conceived only by comparison with another relation conceived in comparison with the first, such as fatherhood and sonship. “This relation” is called “the relative in the strict sense,” just as it is called relation. “The whole composed of it”—the strict relative—“and its subject,” such as a father qualified by fatherhood or a son qualified by sonship, is called “the relative in the customary sense.” “The two” reciprocal “relations may agree” on both sides, as in brotherhood, “or may differ”—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I275.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I276.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I276.lower.01","volume":1,"page":276,"physical_pdf_page":289,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"وقد لا يكون كما في الزيادة والنقصان (قوله أي تعقل كل بالقياس إلى الآخر الخ) الأولى أن يقول أي الحكم بإضافة كل من المضافين إلى الآخر من حيث هو مضاف كما في شرح المقاصد فإن الانعكاس بهذا المعنى هو الذي يتصف بالافتقار إلى حرف النسبة والاستغناء عنه دون ما ذكره الشارح مد ظله فليتأمل","english":"—or it may not be determinate, as in greaterness and lesserness. On “Conceiving each in comparison with the other, etc.” It would be preferable to say: “Judging each of the two relatives to stand in relation to the other precisely insofar as it is relative,” as in Sharḥ al-Maqāṣid. Reciprocity in this sense admits of needing a relational particle or dispensing with it, unlike the account given by the commentator—may his protection endure. Consider this.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I276.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I275.lower.06","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I276.lower.02","volume":1,"page":276,"physical_pdf_page":289,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله أي النسبة) أي قد يفتقر التعبير من عروض النسبة إلى حرف نسبة وهي المراد بالرابطة وذلك فيما إذا لم يكن لأحد المتضايفين لفظ موضوع يدل على الإضافة للتضاد مثل جناح الطير فإنه وإن كان لفظان دالان كذلك لكن الطير المضاف إليه ليس كذلك فيحتاج في التعبير عن عروض الإضافة له إلى كلمة ذي الجناح ومثل علم العالم فإن المضاف إليه وإن كان دالا على الإضافة لكن المضاف ليس كذلك فيحتاج في ذلك التعبير إلى لفظة العالم وقد لا يفتقر التعبير إلى حرف النسبة مطلقا كما إذا كان لكل منهما لفظ موضوع دال على ذلك كالأب والابن","english":"On “The relation”: expressing the predication of a relation may require a relational expression; that is what is meant by the connective. This occurs when one of the correlatives lacks a conventional term indicating the relation, as with a bird’s wing: although “wing” is such a relational term, “bird,” to which it is related, is not. To express the relation’s predication of the bird, the expression “possessor of a wing” is needed. Likewise with “the knower’s knowledge”: although “knower,” the second term, indicates a relation, “knowledge,” the first, does not; [the printed explanation of the expression required here is insecure]. Conversely, no relational particle may be needed at all when each has a conventional term indicating the relation, as with “father” and “son.”","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I276.jpg","stage":69,"readings":[{"locus":"فإنه وإن كان لفظان دالان / إلى لفظة العالم","status":"The short wing example and, especially, the term completing the knowledge example need checking. The apparent repetition of العالم is not silently harmonised."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I276.lower.03","volume":1,"page":276,"physical_pdf_page":289,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله كالعالمية إلى العلم الخ) المراد من العلم هو الصفة الحقيقية التي مر أنها من","english":"On “As being a knower depends upon knowledge, etc.” “Knowledge” means the real attribute which, as explained earlier, belongs to—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I276.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I277.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I276.ruled.01","volume":1,"page":276,"physical_pdf_page":289,"layer":"ruled","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله والانعكاس) أي الحكم بإضافة كل إلى الآخر كما هو اللازم لاعتبار الحرف وعلى تقدير تفسيره بالتعقل يحتاج إلى تأويل اه منه","english":"On “Reciprocity”: that is, judging each to be related to the other, as consideration of the particle requires. If it is instead explained as conception, an interpretation is needed. End. [Signed:] “From him.”","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I276.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I276.upper.01","volume":1,"page":276,"physical_pdf_page":289,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"كالأبوة والبنوة (والانعكاس) أي تعقل كل بالقياس إلى الآخر ثم (قد يستغنى عن) اعتبار (حرف) النسبة كالكبير والصغير (وقد يفتقر) إليه إما على تساوي الحرف في الجانبين كقولك العبد عبد للمولى والمولى مولى للعبد أو على اختلافه كقولك العالم عالم بالمعلوم والمعلوم معلوم للعالم (وقد يفتقر عروضها) أي النسبة إلى (رابطة) كذي الجناح للطير لأن الجناح اسم لأحد المتضايفين مأخوذ مع إضافته وليس للمضاف الآخر أعني الطير اسم كذلك فاعتبروه بلفظ دال على النسبة وهو ذو الجناح","english":"—as in fatherhood and sonship. “Reciprocity” means conceiving each in comparison with the other. One “may dispense with” considering “a particle” of relation, as in “the greater” and “the smaller”; or “may require” it. The particle may be the same on both sides, as in “The slave is a slave of the master, and the master is a master of the slave”; or it may differ, as in “The knower knows the known, and the known is known to the knower.” “Its predication”—the predication of the relation—“may require a connective,” as in describing a bird as “possessor of a wing.” “Wing” names one of the correlatives taken with its relation, but the other correlative—the bird—has no such name. They therefore express it with a term indicating the relation: “possessor of a wing.”","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I276.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I275.upper.02","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I276.upper.02","volume":1,"page":276,"physical_pdf_page":289,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(وقد يكون) عروضها (لصفة في الطرفين) كعروض العاشقية المفتقر إلى صفة الإدراك والمعشوقية إلى الجمال (أو في أحدهما) كالعالمية إلى العلم بخلاف المعلومية (وتعرض) الإضافة (لكل موجود) فالواجب كالأول","english":"“Its predication may depend on an attribute in both termini,” as being a lover depends upon apprehension and being beloved upon beauty; “or in one of them,” as being a knower depends upon knowledge, unlike being known. “Relation occurs in every existent”: in the Necessary, for example, as “the First”—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I276.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I277.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I277.lower.01","volume":1,"page":277,"physical_pdf_page":290,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"الكيف لا المعنى المصدري الذي يفتقر كل من العالمية والمعلومية إليه فتدبر","english":"—quality, not the verbal-noun meaning upon which both being a knower and being known depend. Reflect.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I277.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I276.lower.03","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I277.lower.02","volume":1,"page":277,"physical_pdf_page":290,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله محصلة الخ) المراد من تحصيلها إضافتها إلى المعروض وتقييدها بخصوصها وبهذا تتنوع الإضافة","english":"On “Determinate, etc.” Determining the relation means assigning it to its subject and restricting it by the subject’s particular character; through this, relation is diversified.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I277.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I277.lower.03","volume":1,"page":277,"physical_pdf_page":290,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله وإنما الافتراق بين الذاتين) فذاتا المضافين قد يوجد كل منهما بدون الآخر","english":"On “The separation is between the two subjects.” Each subject of the correlatives may exist without the other—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I277.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I278.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I277.upper.01","volume":1,"page":277,"physical_pdf_page":290,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"والجوهر كالأب والكم كالأقل والكيف كالأحمر والأين كالأعلى والمتى كالأقدم والإضافة كالأقرب والوضع كالأشد انحناء وانتصابا والملك كالأكسى والأعرى والفعل كالأقطع والانفعال كالأشد تسخنا (ويتكافأ الطرفان) للإضافة (في التحصيل والإطلاق) بمعنى إذا كانت الإضافة في طرف محصلة ففي الطرف الآخر كذلك وإن كانت مطلقة ففي الطرف الآخر كذلك فالنصف المطلق في مقابلة الضعف المطلق وهذا النصف في مقابلة هذا الضعف","english":"—in substance, as “father”; in quantity, as “the lesser”; in quality, as “the redder”; in place, as “the higher”; in time, as “the earlier”; in relation itself, as “the nearer”; in position, as “more bent” or “more upright”; in having, as “more clothed” or “more naked”; in acting, as “more cutting”; and in being acted upon, as “more heated.” “The two termini” of relation “correspond in determination and unrestrictedness”: if the relation on one side is determinate, so is that on the other; if unrestricted, so is the other. Halfness without restriction corresponds to doubleness without restriction; this particular half corresponds to this particular double.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I277.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I276.upper.02","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I277.upper.02","volume":1,"page":277,"physical_pdf_page":290,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(و) كذا في (الوجود والعدم ذهنا وخارجا قوة وفعلا) فكلما وجد أحدهما في الذهن أو الخارج قوة وفعلا وجد الآخر كذلك وكلما عدم أحدهما في أحدهما عدم الآخر فيه فإن قيل المتقدم والمتأخر بحسب الزمان متضايفان مع أنهما لا يوجدان معا قلنا التضايف إنما هو بين مفهوميهما لأنهما عبارة عن مفهومي التقدم والتأخر وهما أمران ذهنيان لا انفكاك بينهما في الذهن وإنما الافتراق بين الذاتين","english":"“And” likewise in “existence and nonexistence, mentally and extramentally, potentially and actually.” Whenever one exists in mind or outside it, potentially or actually, the other does likewise; and whenever one is absent in either, the other is absent there. If it is objected that the temporally earlier and later are correlatives although they do not coexist, we reply: the correlation is between their concepts, namely priority and posteriority. These are mental matters inseparable in the mind. The separation is between the two subjects.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I277.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I277.upper.03","volume":1,"page":277,"physical_pdf_page":290,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(و) ما ذكر وإن كان مشعرا بأن الإضافة قد توجد في الخارج لكن (الجمهور) من المتكلمين كلهم وبعض الحكماء (على أنه أمر اعتباري) لا تحقق له في الخارج أصلا (وإلا لتسلسل) لأن وجودها في الخارج يستلزم كونها في محل و(الحلول) في المحل (إضافة) بين الحال والمحل و(لها حلول) أيضا فينقل الكلام إليه وهلم جرا بخلاف ما إذا كانت أمرا اعتباريا","english":"“And,” although what was said may suggest that relation exists outside the mind, “the majority”—all the theologians and some philosophers—“hold it to be a matter of consideration,” with no extramental reality at all. “Otherwise there would be an infinite regress”: extramental existence entails being in a subject; “inherence” in a subject is “a relation” between what inheres and its subject; and “it, too, has an inherence.” The same question then recurs indefinitely. This does not follow if relation is a matter of consideration.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I277.jpg","stage":69,"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I277.upper.04","volume":1,"page":277,"physical_pdf_page":290,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(و) أيضا لو كانت موجودة (لزم لا تناهي أوصاف كل عدد بحسب ما له من الإضافة إلى ما عداه) من الأعداد الغير المتناهية فإن اثنين مثلا نصف الأربعة وثلث الستة وربع الثمانية وهكذا إلى النهاية (وقد يجاب بأن) غاية ذلك امتناع أن يوجد كل إضافة لا امتناع البعض المذكورات يلزم على تقدير أن يكون كل ما هو من","english":"“And,” furthermore, if relations existed, “the attributes of each number would be infinite according to its relations to the others,” namely the infinitely many numbers. Two, for example, is half of four, a third of six, a quarter of eight, and so on without end. “It may be answered that” this establishes at most that not every relation can exist, not that some cannot exist. The stated [consequences] follow on the supposition that everything belonging to—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I277.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I278.upper.01","readings":[{"locus":"لا امتناع البعض المذكورات","status":"A connector between البعض and المذكورات is unclear. The quantifier distinction is secure: denying all relations collectively does not deny each individually."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I278.lower.01","volume":1,"page":278,"physical_pdf_page":291,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"كالأب والابن وقد يوجد أحدهما بدون الآخر من غير عكس كالعالم والعلم وقد يمتنع وجود كل بدون الآخر كالعلة ومعلولها الخاص","english":"—as with a father and a son. One may exist without the other, but not conversely, as with the knower and knowledge; or neither may exist without the other, as with a cause and its specific effect.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","source_image":"source_images/I278.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I277.lower.03","proof_notes":["Lower enlargement resolves معلولها الخاص. The example concerns the cause and its proper/specific effect, not a plural adjective التامين.","Upper, ruled, and full lower registers reread against enlarged source crops. One short Arabic expression remains marked; not independent witness collation."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I278.lower.02","volume":1,"page":278,"physical_pdf_page":291,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله كانت تابعة لها في الأحكام الخ) قد يقال إن هذا ينافي ما سبق من أن تنوع المعروضات لا يوجب تنوع العوارض فتفهم","english":"On “They follow them in these determinations, etc.” One might say that this conflicts with the earlier statement that diversity of subjects does not require diversity of their accidents. Understand this.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","source_image":"source_images/I278.jpg","stage":69,"proof_notes":["Upper, ruled, and full lower registers reread against enlarged source crops. One short Arabic expression remains marked; not independent witness collation."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I278.lower.03","volume":1,"page":278,"physical_pdf_page":291,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله كثيرا ما يسأل بمتى الخ) اعلم أن الوقوع في الزمان قد يكون بمعنى أن للشيء هوية اتصالية تنطبق على الزمان ولا يمكن أن تحصل الآنية وهو معنى الحصول على التدريج وذلك كالحركات وما يشبهها وقد يكون بمعنى أنه لا يوجد له في ذلك الزمان آن إلا ويكون ذلك الشيء حاصلا فيه فيكون حصوله دفعة لكن على استمرار الآنات إلا أن وقوع الشيء في الآن الذي هو طرف الزمان يدل على أن الآن أمر موجود لامتناع وقوع الشيء في غير الموجود مع أنه لا خفاء في أنه لا تحقق لطرف الشيء إلا بعد انقطاعه وانقسامه بالفعل والزمان إنما ينقسم بالفرض والوهم فقط وأيضا لو وجد الآن ولا شك","english":"On “One often asks ‘When?’, etc.” Know that being in time may mean that a thing has a continuous identity corresponding to time and cannot occur instantaneously; this is what gradual occurrence means, as with motions and similar things. Or it may mean that no instant in that time lacks the thing’s presence: it occurs all at once, but persists through the instants. However, a thing’s occurrence at an instant, the extremity of time, indicates that the instant is an existent, since nothing can occur in what is nonexistent. Yet it is evident that a thing’s extremity is not actual except after interruption and actual division, whereas time is divided only by supposition and imagination. Furthermore, if an instant existed—and undoubtedly—","reading_status":"second_visual_read","source_image":"source_images/I278.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I279.lower.01","readings":[{"locus":"لا يمكن أن تحصل الآنية","status":"Exact form of the short expression for instantaneous occurrence remains slightly uncertain; contrast with gradual occurrence is clear."}],"proof_notes":["Lower enlargement resolves إلا أن (however), not a negation لا أن. The gloss raises an objection about the reality of the instant; the earlier denial reversed that move. The final ولا شك joins I279 أنه على التقضي.","Upper, ruled, and full lower registers reread against enlarged source crops. One short Arabic expression remains marked; not independent witness collation."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I278.ruled.01","volume":1,"page":278,"physical_pdf_page":291,"layer":"ruled","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله بل تابعة لمعروضاتها) بمعنى أنها ملحوظة في تعقلها اه منه","english":"On “But dependent upon their subjects”: meaning that [the subjects] are considered in conceiving them. End. [Signed:] “From him.”","reading_status":"second_visual_read","source_image":"source_images/I278.jpg","stage":69,"proof_notes":["Upper, ruled, and full lower registers reread against enlarged source crops. One short Arabic expression remains marked; not independent witness collation."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I278.upper.01","volume":1,"page":278,"physical_pdf_page":291,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"أفراد الإضافة موجودا فاللازم من ذلك هو أن لا يوجد الكل وهذا سلب الكل و(سلب الكل لا يقتضي السلب الكلي) الذي هو المطلوب فإن قيل هي طبيعة واحدة فلا تختلف أفرادها بامتناع الوجود وإمكانه قلنا ممنوع بل هي طبيعة جنسية فلا يمتنع فيها ذلك (والتمسك في وجودها) بأن نقطع بفوقية السماء وتحتية الأرض وأبوة زيد وبنوة عمرو إلى غير ذلك (وإن لم يوجد اعتبار العقل) فيكون كل من ذلك موجودا عينيا لا بمجرد اعتبار العقل (ضعيف) لأن القطع إنما هو بصدق قولنا مثلا هذه السماء فوقنا كما في قولنا زيد أعمى وذلك لا يستدعي وجود الفوقية والعمى لجواز اتصاف الأعيان الخارجية بالأمور الاعتبارية","english":"—the instances of relation exists. What follows is that they do not all exist; this is denial of the whole. “Denying the whole does not entail universal denial,” which is what is sought. If it is said that relation is one nature and its instances therefore cannot differ in the impossibility or possibility of existence, we deny this: it is a generic nature, and such a difference is not impossible within it. “The appeal for their existence”—our certainty that the sky is above, the earth below, Zayd a father and ʿAmr a son, and so forth—“even without the intellect’s consideration,” so that each would be objectively existent rather than merely considered, “is weak.” Our certainty concerns the truth of a statement such as “This sky is above us,” just as with “Zayd is blind.” That does not require above-ness or blindness to be existent: extramental things can be qualified by matters of consideration.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","source_image":"source_images/I278.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I277.upper.04","proof_notes":["Whole upper register enlarged: والتمسك, إلى غير ذلك, كما في قولنا زيد أعمى are visible. Removes invented or malformed first readings without altering the secure quantifier argument.","Upper, ruled, and full lower registers reread against enlarged source crops. One short Arabic expression remains marked; not independent witness collation."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I278.upper.02","volume":1,"page":278,"physical_pdf_page":291,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"(ثم إنها في جنسيتها ونوعيتها وشخصيتها وتضادها تابعة للمعروضات) بناء على ما ذكر ومن أن الإضافات لكونها بالطبع غير مستقلة بأنفسها بل تابعة لمعروضاتها كانت تابعة لها في الأحكام والمراد بالمعروضات المقولات التي يعرضها الإضافات بحسب التعقل كالمتى (والمتى هي النسبة إلى الزمان أو) إلى (الآن) فإنه كثيرا ما يسأل بمتى","english":"“Furthermore, in its generic, specific and individual character, and in its contrariety, it follows its subjects.” This rests on the foregoing and on the fact that relations are by nature not independent in themselves, but dependent upon their subjects; they therefore follow them in these determinations. “Subjects” means the categories to which relations accrue in conception, such as “when.” “When is relation to time, or” to “an instant,” for one often asks “When?”—","reading_status":"second_visual_read","source_image":"source_images/I278.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I279.upper.01","proof_notes":["Enlarged upper image clearly reads بالطبع, “by nature,” not the meaningless first-pass المطابخ.","Upper, ruled, and full lower registers reread against enlarged source crops. One short Arabic expression remains marked; not independent witness collation."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I279.lower.01","volume":1,"page":279,"physical_pdf_page":292,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"أنه على التقضي دون البقاء وأن حدوث عدمه لا يكون إلا في آن يلزم تتالي الآنين المستلزم للجزء وقد يجاب بأن عدم الآن يكون في جميع الذي بعد وجوده لكن بالمعنى الثاني أعني أنه لا يوجد في ذلك الزمان آن إلا وذلك العدم حاصل فيه لا بالمعنى الأول الذي هو على سبيل الانطباق والتدريج حتى يلزم كون الآن زمانيا ورد بأن كلامنا في حدوث ذلك العدم لا في نفسه ولا شك أن حدوثه آني وليس في جميع الزمان الذي بعده ومن البين أن هذا الآن مغاير لآن الوجود فيلزم تتالي الآنين كذا قرره بعض المحققين","english":"—it is passing rather than enduring, and the origination of its nonexistence can occur only at an instant, two instants would immediately succeed, entailing an indivisible part. It may be answered that the instant’s nonexistence obtains throughout what follows its existence, but in the second sense: no instant in that time lacks the nonexistence in question. It is not in the first sense of correspondence and gradual occurrence, which would make the instant temporal. It is objected that we are discussing the origination of that nonexistence, not the nonexistence itself. Its origination is unquestionably instantaneous, not spread throughout the subsequent time; and this instant plainly differs from the instant of existence. Immediate succession of two instants therefore follows. Such is the formulation of some investigators.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","source_image":"source_images/I279.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I278.lower.03","proof_notes":["Incoming join from I278 now correctly reads ولا شك أنه, “and undoubtedly it”; rest of this unit not newly whole-source-proofed."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I279.lower.02","volume":1,"page":279,"physical_pdf_page":292,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(وأقول) لا شك أن الحركة بمعنى التوسط موجودة عندهم دفعة على الاستمرار دون الاستقرار وليست لها هوية اتصالية منطبقة على الزمان كالحركة بمعنى القطع على ما مر وحينئذ لا يخلو إما أن يكون معنى وجودها دفعة على الاستمرار هو أنها جزئي يحدث في الآن ويستمر إلى آخر المسافة تجدد الأمثال فلا شك أن حدوثه يكون في آن وحدوث عدمه في آن آخر وأن عدمه مغاير لآن حدوثه فيلزم تتالي الآنين وإما أن يكون معناه هو أنها تحدث دفعة وتستمر بنفسها لا بتجدد الأمثال إلى آخر المسافة بمعنى أنه لا يوجد في زمانها المنطبق على مسافتها آن إلا وهي موجودة فيه فليكن الآن الذي يقع فيه الشيء وجوده بمعنى أنه لا توجد الحركة بمعنى التوسط التي هي دفعة في شيء من حدود المسافة إلا وذلك الآن ظرف لها ووجوده بهذا المعنى لا يوجب طرد العدم عليه والاجتماع أجزاء الزمان في الوجود وحينئذ كما أن سيلان تلك الحركة الموجودة في حدود المسافة يرسم الحركة الكلية الممتدة المتوهمة فكذلك سيلان ذلك الآن بالنسبة إليها في تلك الحدود يرسم الزمان الممتد المتوهم فتأمله فإنه من بدائعنا وأجعله منتظما في سلك نظائره لكن الناظر المدقق في اضطراب كلام الفلاسفة يقضي إلى الحكم بأن الزمان أمر لا تحقق له كما عليه المتكلمون","english":"I say: motion as intermediacy, on their account, undoubtedly exists all at once with continuity of persistence, but without settled stability. It does not have a continuous identity corresponding to time in the way motion as traversal does, as explained earlier. “Existing all at once with persistence” must mean one of two things. Either it is a particular arising at an instant and continuing to the distance’s end by the renewal of like instances. Its origination then occurs at one instant, and the origination of its nonexistence at another; the instant of its nonexistence differs from that of its origination, and immediately successive instants follow. Or it arises all at once and persists in its own identity, not by renewed like instances, to the distance’s end: no instant in its time, corresponding to its distance, lacks its existence. Let the instant in which something occurs likewise exist in this sense: instantaneous motion as intermediacy is never present at a boundary of the distance without that instant being its temporal receptacle. Existence in this sense does not require either [the succession of] nonexistence upon it or the simultaneous existence of temporal parts. Just as the flowing of this motion, existent at the boundaries of the distance, traces the imagined, extended motion as a whole, so the flowing of that instant in relation to it at those boundaries traces imagined, extended time. Consider this: it is one of our original insights; place it among its counterparts. Nevertheless, the exacting reader who examines the instability of the philosophers’ formulations is led to judge time to have no [extramental] reality, as the theologians maintain.","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I279.jpg","stage":69,"readings":[{"locus":"وأن عدمه مغاير لآن حدوثه / فليكن الآن ... وجوده / طرد العدم عليه","status":"Several connectors in this long argument remain provisional. The two models—renewed like instances versus persisting numerical identity—and the proposed flowing-instant analogy are preserved distinctly; no modern model is substituted."}],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I279.lower.03","volume":1,"page":279,"physical_pdf_page":292,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"ثم اعلم أن مقولة متى هي النسبة إلى الزمان كما أن مقولة الأين هي النسبة إلى المكان وهي قد تكون حقيقية ككون الشيء في زمان لا يفضل عنه كحدوث المطر مثلا في ساعة معينة وقد تكون غير حقيقية ككون الكسوف في شهر كذا وهكذا الأين إلا أن الحقيقي من متى يجوز فيه الاشتراك بأن","english":"Know, further, that the category “when” is relation to time, just as “where” is relation to place. It may be exact, as when something occupies a time not exceeding it—for example, the occurrence of rain during a specified hour—or inexact, as when an eclipse occurs in such-and-such a month. The same applies to “where.” Exact “when,” however, admits of being shared, in that—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I279.jpg","stage":69,"join_out":"I280.lower.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I279.upper.01","volume":1,"page":279,"physical_pdf_page":292,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"عن وقوع الشيء في طرف الزمان وهو الآن كالوصول إلى منتهى المسافة (والوضع هو كون الجسم بحيث تكون لأجزائه نسبة فيما بينها أو) يكون لها نسبة (إلى الأمور الخارجة عنها) كالقيام هيئة للإنسان بحسب انتصابه وهي نسبة فيما بين أجزائه وبحسب كون رأسه من فوق ورجله من تحت وهي نسبة إلى الأمور الخارجية ولهذا","english":"—about something’s occurrence at a temporal boundary, which is an instant, such as arrival at the endpoint of a distance. “Position is a body’s being such that its parts have a relation among themselves, or” a relation “to things external to them.” Standing, for example, is a configuration of a human being in respect of uprightness—a relation among the parts—and in respect of the head’s being above and the feet below—a relation to external things. For this reason—","reading_status":"first_visual_transcription_and_translation","source_image":"source_images/I279.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I278.upper.02","join_out":"I280.upper.01","source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I280.lower.01","volume":1,"page":280,"physical_pdf_page":293,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"تتصف أشياء كثيرة بالسكون في زمان معين بخلاف الأين وهو ظاهر","english":"—many things can be qualified by rest during a single specified time, unlike [exact] “where.” This is evident.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","source_image":"source_images/I280.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I279.lower.03","proof_notes":["Whole upper and lower registers, closing notice, and I279 incoming joins reread against enlarged source images I280_upper_full.png and I280_lower_full.png. Self-proof, not independent collation."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I280.lower.02","volume":1,"page":280,"physical_pdf_page":293,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله وضعا آخر الخ) فالمحيط على الإطلاق يكون له الوضع بحسب الأمور الداخلة فقط والمحاط على الإطلاق بالعكس والمتوسط بالاعتبارين","english":"On “Another position, etc.” What is absolutely encompassing has position only in respect of internal things; what is absolutely encompassed has it conversely [in respect of external things]; what is intermediate has position under both considerations.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","source_image":"source_images/I280.jpg","stage":69,"proof_notes":["Magnified full lower image confirms فالمحيط, والمحاط and بالاعتبارين; the earlier الحائط was a transcription error.","Whole upper and lower registers, closing notice, and I279 incoming joins reread against enlarged source images I280_upper_full.png and I280_lower_full.png. Self-proof, not independent collation."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I280.lower.03","volume":1,"page":280,"physical_pdf_page":293,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله احترازا عن المكان الخ) أقول لا شك في خروج المكان بمعنى البعد المفطور لعدم انتقاله بانتقال المتمكن وأما إذا كان بمعنى السطح الباطن أو المكان العرفي فلا يخرج إذ قد ينتقل بانتقال المتمكن اللهم إلا أن يقال المراد أن انتقاله يكون موجبا لانتقاله بمعنى أنه كلما انتقل المحصور انتقل الحاصر مثل ذلك الانتقال فليتأمل ولعل لهذا نقل عن الشيخ الرئيس أنه قال وأما أنا فلا أعرف هذه المقولة حق المعرفة اللهم إلا أنها تسمى بالجدة وبمقولة له كما يقال له عمامة وله قميص إلى غير ذلك وهي قد تكون ذاتية كنسبة الهرة إلى إهابها وقد تكون عرضية كنسبة الإنسان إلى قميصه","english":"On “This excludes place, etc.” I say: place understood as naturally apprehended extension is certainly excluded, since it does not move when its occupant moves. But place understood as the inner surface, or place in ordinary usage, is not excluded, since it may move with its occupant—unless what is intended is that the occupant’s movement necessitates its movement: whenever the enclosed thing moves, the enclosing thing undergoes a like movement. Consider this. Perhaps this is why the Chief Master is reported to have said: “As for me, I do not properly understand this category, except that it is called having (jiddah) and the category ‘has,’ as when one says he has a turban or a shirt,” and so forth. It may be intrinsic, as a cat’s relation to its skin, or accidental, as a human being’s relation to a shirt.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","source_image":"source_images/I280.jpg","stage":69,"editorial":"“Chief Master” is the printed designation. The reported statement is translated as transmitted here; no independent Avicennian witness has been collated in this stage.","proof_notes":["Enlarged source resolves إذ قد ينتقل (may move), ولعل لهذا, and بالجدة وبمقولة له, correcting overstatement and the earlier mistaken الملكة. No independent witness is used.","Whole upper and lower registers, closing notice, and I279 incoming joins reread against enlarged source images I280_upper_full.png and I280_lower_full.png. Self-proof, not independent collation."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I280.lower.04","volume":1,"page":280,"physical_pdf_page":293,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله كمسخن) أي في الإجمال (قوله حالة غير قارة هي التأثير الخ) فهي مستمرة غير مستقرة ووافق ذلك التفسير بعضهم أن يفعل بالتغيير والتحريك وأن ينفعل بالتغير والتحرك ويصير أنواع هذين الجنسين أنواع الحركة كالنمو في الكم والتكون في الجوهر وهكذا ثم المحققون على أن ثبوت هاتين المقولتين إنما هو في الذهن إذ لو وجدتا في الخارج لافتقر كل منهما إلى مؤثر فله تأثير آخر ضرورة امتناع كون التأثير نفس الأثر على تقدير كونهما من الأمور الخارجية وحينئذ يلزم التسلسل في الموجودات مع كونها محصورة بين حاصرين فتدبر","english":"On “Like something heating”: that is, in general outline. On “A nonstable condition, namely its affecting, etc.” It is persistent without being settled. This agrees with the explanation given by some: acting is changing and moving [something], and being acted upon is being changed and moved. The species of these two genera then become the species of motion, such as growth in quantity and coming-to-be in substance, and similarly elsewhere. The investigators hold, however, that these two categories are established only in the mind. If they existed extramentally, each would require an efficient cause, and that cause would have another effecting; effecting cannot be identical with its effect if both are extramental things. An infinite regress of existents would then follow, although they are confined between two limits. Reflect.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","source_image":"source_images/I280.jpg","stage":69,"proof_notes":["Magnification resolves كمسخن, في الإجمال, التكون, and especially لافتقر كل منهما إلى مؤثر. The latter positively asserts need for an efficient cause; the earlier لا تقوم misread the regress. The short ووافق ... التفسير clause is read as correspondence of the explanations.","Whole upper and lower registers, closing notice, and I279 incoming joins reread against enlarged source images I280_upper_full.png and I280_lower_full.png. Self-proof, not independent collation."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I280.lower.05","volume":1,"page":280,"physical_pdf_page":293,"layer":"lower","attribution":"unassigned printed annotation","arabic":"(قوله قد يسمى أثرا وانفعالا الخ) وكذا الحال الحاصل للفاعل قبل التأثير وبعده ليس من هذا القبيل وإن كان قد يسمى بذلك كقوة النار تسمى إحراقا كذا قيل","english":"On “Although it may be called an effect or an affection, etc.” Likewise, the condition obtained by the agent before and after its affecting is not of this kind, although it may be called by such a name—as fire’s capacity is called “burning.” Such is the account given.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","source_image":"source_images/I280.jpg","stage":69,"proof_notes":["The enlarged final line reads قوة النار and إحراقا, not قوة النجار. The previous carpenter example was a transcription-derived error.","Whole upper and lower registers, closing notice, and I279 incoming joins reread against enlarged source images I280_upper_full.png and I280_lower_full.png. Self-proof, not independent collation."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I280.paratext.01","volume":1,"page":280,"physical_pdf_page":293,"layer":"paratext","attribution":"printed volume-closing notice","arabic":"تم الجزء الأول ويليه الجزء الثاني أوله الباب الرابع في الجواهر","english":"The first part is complete. The second part follows, beginning with “Chapter Four: On Substances.”","reading_status":"second_visual_read","source_image":"source_images/I280.jpg","stage":69,"editorial":"This is the source’s colophon, not a claim that the present translation project is complete.","proof_notes":["Whole upper and lower registers, closing notice, and I279 incoming joins reread against enlarged source images I280_upper_full.png and I280_lower_full.png. Self-proof, not independent collation."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false},{"id":"I280.upper.01","volume":1,"page":280,"physical_pdf_page":293,"layer":"upper","attribution":"Taftazani lemmata within Sanandaji commentary","arabic":"يسمى الانعكاس وضعا آخر (والملك هو نسبة الجسم إلى ما حصره) كنسبة البدن إلى الثوب الشامل له (أو لبعضه) كنسبته إلى الخاتم والعمامة والخف والقميص وغيرها (و) يكون الحاصر بحيث (ينتقل بانتقاله) أي بانتقال الجسم احترازا عن المكان فإنه لا ينتقل بانتقال المتمكن (وأن يفعل هو تأثير الشيء في الشيء ما دام سالكا) ولذا عبر به دون الفعل كحال المسخن ما دام يسخن فإن له ما دام يسخن حالة غير قارة هي التأثير التسخيني (وأن ينفعل هو التأثر عن الغير كذلك) كحال المتسخن ما دام يسخن فإن له حالة غير قارة هي التأثر التسخني","english":"—inversion is called another position. “Having is the relation of a body to what encloses it,” as the body’s relation to a garment enveloping it, “or [encloses] part of it,” as its relation to a ring, turban, shoe, shirt and the like. “And” the enclosing thing must be such that “it moves with it”—with the body. This excludes place, which does not move with what occupies it. “Acting is a thing’s affecting another thing while the process is under way.” Hence this expression is used rather than [the completed noun] “action”: consider the condition of something heating another thing while it heats. During that time it has a nonstable condition, namely its heating influence. “Being acted upon is undergoing an effect from another in that same manner”: consider something being heated while it warms. It has a nonstable condition, namely undergoing warming.","reading_status":"second_visual_read","source_image":"source_images/I280.jpg","stage":69,"join_in":"I279.upper.01","editorial":"The base-print phrase أو لبعضه is inclusive, “or part of it.” Rings and partial coverings are examples included by the definition, not exclusions. “Having” here translates the category milk/jiddah, not legal ownership.","proof_notes":["Whole upper and lower registers, closing notice, and I279 incoming joins reread against enlarged source images I280_upper_full.png and I280_lower_full.png. Self-proof, not independent collation."],"source_kind":"full_typed_arabic_provisional","all_printed_clauses_literally_translated":false,"independent_second_proof":false,"textual_witness_collation":false}]);